/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"note-level-7","number":7,"title":"Instruments","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"7.1","modules":[{"id":"note-m-7-1-101","code":"7.1","title":"7.1","blurb":"7.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l7-1-1","title":"7.1.1 — A Track Preset is an instrument plus two effects","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.1 — A Track Preset is an instrument plus two effects","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Treat every track’s sound as one package: an instrument plus exactly two audio effects."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Instrument View and you see a preset name at the top, then a row of <strong>Track Preset</strong> tabs. That package is the Track Preset. The manual’s definition is strict: an instrument with adjustable parameters <strong>and two audio effects</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"The instrument occupies the first tab (and, on samplers, a Sample tab). The two rightmost tabs are the effects. On Drift, Melodic Sampler, and Wavetable those tabs are <strong>FX1</strong> then <strong>FX2</strong> — serial, FX1 into FX2. On a Drum Rack they are <strong>Pad FX</strong> (per selected pad, with <strong>Send Amount</strong>) and <strong>Kit FX</strong> (the whole rack)."},{"type":"prose","html":"A new track loads a random Track Preset. You can swap the whole package by tapping the preset name, or swap only an effect by tapping that effect’s name. You are not building a Live-style device chain of arbitrary length. Two effects is the surface."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is why browsing “a bass” also browses its delay and saturator. If the instrument is right and the space is wrong, change the effect tabs. If both are wrong, change the Track Preset."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open any track and read the preset name at the top of Instrument View.","Tap through every Track Preset tab and count them. Find the instrument tab and the two effect tabs.","On a synth or sampler track, name <strong>FX1</strong> and <strong>FX2</strong>. On a Drum Rack, name <strong>Pad FX</strong> and <strong>Kit FX</strong>.","Tap the preset name, pick a different preset, tap <strong>Done</strong>. Confirm both the instrument and the effects changed.","Tap one effect name only, pick a different effect, tap <strong>Done</strong>. Confirm the instrument stayed.","Repeat on a second track type from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is inside a Track Preset? <strong>Answer:</strong> One instrument plus two audio effects.","How do Drum Rack effect tabs differ from Drift/Sampler/Wavetable? <strong>Answer:</strong> Drums use <strong>Pad FX</strong> + <strong>Kit FX</strong>. The others use serial <strong>FX1</strong> → <strong>FX2</strong>.","Can you add a third effect slot on the track? <strong>Answer:</strong> Not on the Track Preset surface. Two is the documented count. (Main-track FX are a different, later topic.)"],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1 Track Presets; §7.4 Audio Effects in Track Presets"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one scene, identify the instrument and both effects on every track. Write the six names down. Swap only the effect that is washing out a drum kit."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-2","title":"7.1.2 — Instrument icons recognition drill","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.2 — Instrument icons recognition drill","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Name the four Note instruments from their icons without opening the browser."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The first Track Preset tab shows a distinct icon for whichever instrument is loaded. The manual lists four, and only four:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Drift</strong> — subtractive synthesizer.","<strong>Drum Rack</strong> — 16 pads; each pad is a Drum Sampler plus a pad-effect send; one effect for the whole rack.","<strong>Melodic Sampler</strong> — one sample, repitched across the pads.","<strong>Wavetable</strong> — synthesizer that uses wavetable-based oscillators."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"You do not need oscillator theory here. You need to recognize the icon so you know which parameter set you are about to touch. Drift and Wavetable look like synths but they are not the same device. Sampler and Drum Rack both play samples but they are not the same device."},{"type":"prose","html":"The icon is also how you know what will happen when the Set opens in Live. A Wavetable Track Preset becomes a locked rack in Live Lite, Intro, or Standard (macros only). Full Wavetable is Suite or a Pack. Drift, Drum Rack, and Melodic Sampler do not carry that Suite lock."},{"type":"prose","html":"Drill until a glance at the first tab tells you “this is drums” vs “this is a pitched sample” vs “this is Drift” vs “this is Wavetable.”"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open each default track in a new Set and look at the first-tab icon only.","Say the instrument name before you tap the preset name to confirm.","Browse until you have loaded all four types at least once (swap presets if a new Set did not include Wavetable).","Return to Session View and identify each track from the header/icon without opening Instrument View if the icon is visible; if not, open the first tab only.","Write the four names from memory.","Open one track at random and name it in one second."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Name the four instruments. <strong>Answer:</strong> Drift, Drum Rack, Melodic Sampler, Wavetable.","Which one is 16 pads, each a Drum Sampler? <strong>Answer:</strong> Drum Rack.","Which one becomes a locked rack in Live Standard? <strong>Answer:</strong> A Wavetable-from-Note preset (Suite-only device). Macros still work."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1 Track Presets (instrument list and icons)","Using Note Presets in Live (Wavetable locked rack in Lite/Intro/Standard)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Ten rapid opens: jump track to track, name the icon, close. Misses restart the count. Stop at ten correct."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-3","title":"7.1.3 — Core Library categories","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.3 — Core Library categories","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open the Core Library folder and browse presets by category instead of scrolling one long list."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tap the preset name on a track to open the browser. You start in the current category — the manual’s picture is the Mallets list. That is not the whole library. Use the back navigation to reach the <strong>Core Library</strong> folder. There you see the full list of preset categories."},{"type":"prose","html":"Categories are how Note groups sounds for sketching: drums, basses, leads, pads, mallets, and the rest of the factory set. The local manual shows the folder and the category list but does <strong>not</strong> print an exhaustive category table. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for a complete official list of category names. Use the names you see on screen."},{"type":"prose","html":"Core Library presets already include the instrument and the two effects. Tapping a name previews it. Playing the pads tests it. <strong>Done</strong> loads it. You can also search and filter (next lessons) once you know where the folder lives."},{"type":"prose","html":"User presets do not live here. They live in <strong>User Library</strong>, organized by instrument category. Do not look for your saved bass in Core Library."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Tap the Track Preset name to open the browser.","Navigate back until you see <strong>Core Library</strong>. Open it.","Scroll the category list. Write down at least six category names exactly as shown.","Open one category, tap two presets, and play the pads under the list.","Tap <strong>Done</strong> on a preset you will actually use.","Reopen the browser and return to <strong>Core Library</strong> from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is the full list of factory preset categories? <strong>Answer:</strong> In the <strong>Core Library</strong> folder of the browser.","Does tapping a preset name immediately replace the track? <strong>Answer:</strong> You preview on tap and play pads to test. <strong>Done</strong> loads the selected preset.","Are user presets stored in Core Library? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. They go to <strong>User Library</strong>, by instrument category."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1.1 Browsing Presets","Ableton Note Manual §7.1.2 User Presets"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Pick one category you rarely use. Load three presets from it, keep the one that fits your current Set, and leave the category path in your muscle memory."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-4","title":"7.1.4 — Search bar","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.4 — Search bar","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Find a preset by keyword with the search bar at the top of the browser."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The browser’s top edge has a search bar. Type a keyword and Note looks for matching presets. This is faster than walking Core Library categories when you remember a word from a name — “808,” “pad,” “pluck,” “kite,” whatever string the preset actually contains."},{"type":"prose","html":"Search does not invent a new library. It filters what is already in the browser context you are in. If you are inside one category, notice whether results stay in that category or widen; the manual does not spell out scope. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for whether search is global or folder-local. Test once and trust what you see."},{"type":"prose","html":"You still tap a result to preview, play pads to test, and tap <strong>Done</strong> to load. Search is not a load button."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not search for Live-only device names and expect AUv3 or Suite instruments to appear. Note’s browser holds Note Track Presets and the factory/user content that belongs to them."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Tap the preset name to open the browser.","Tap the search bar at the top.","Type a word you can see in a nearby preset name. Confirm matching results appear.","Tap one result, play the pads, then search a second word (for example a role: bass, lead, kit).","Clear the search and confirm the previous list returns.","Load one searched preset with <strong>Done</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is search? <strong>Answer:</strong> The bar at the top of the browser.","Does typing a name load the preset? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Tap to preview, play pads, then <strong>Done</strong>.","Is search scope (whole library vs current folder) documented? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <code>{type:'gap'}</code>. Check on your device."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1.1 Browsing Presets (search bar)","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (search and favorite samples/presets)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Search three different words related to your working title. Load one hit. If search returns nothing useful, go back to categories — the keyword may not be in any preset name."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-5","title":"7.1.5 — Filters icon","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.5 — Filters icon","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open the <strong>Filters</strong> icon beside the search bar and use it to narrow the preset list."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To the right of the search bar sits the <strong>Filters</strong> icon. Tap it to filter the list by preset type. The manual introduces this control here and then uses the same icon for two documented filters: <strong>Favorite</strong>, and (from Note 1.5) device type."},{"type":"prose","html":"Think of Filters as the structured alternative to search. Search matches letters in names. Filters match a property: this is a favorite, this is a Drift, this is a Drum Rack. You can combine that with browsing a category, but the extracts do not detail every combination. Learn the icon first, then the specific filter lessons."},{"type":"prose","html":"Closing Filters (or clearing the selected filter) returns you to the unfiltered list. The icon does not load a preset. It only changes what the list shows."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you cannot find a preset you know exists, check whether a filter is still on. A leftover <strong>Favorite</strong> filter makes the Core Library look empty."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the Track Preset browser.","Tap the <strong>Filters</strong> icon to the right of the search bar.","Note every filter option you see. Write the labels exactly.","Apply one filter. Scroll the shortened list.","Clear or switch the filter and confirm the full list returns.","Leave Filters closed so the next lesson starts clean."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is the <strong>Filters</strong> icon? <strong>Answer:</strong> To the right of the search bar in the browser.","What does the manual say you can filter for from this icon? <strong>Answer:</strong> Specific preset type. Later lines add Favorite and (1.5) device type.","Does applying a filter load a sound? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It only narrows the list."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1.1 Browsing Presets (Filters icon)","Note Release Notes 1.5 (preset filter by device type)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Filters, apply each option you see for a few seconds, and write a one-line map: filter name → what the list became. Keep that map for the next two lessons."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-6","title":"7.1.6 — Favorites heart","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.6 — Favorites heart","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Heart a Track Preset so you can find it again without remembering its category."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Next to each preset name is a heart icon. Tap it to add that Track Preset to Favorites. Tap the heart again to remove it. The factory preset is unchanged. You are only tagging it."},{"type":"prose","html":"Favorites exist because Core Library is large and random Track Presets on new tracks will not remember your taste. Heart the kick kit you trust, the bass that always sits, the pad you use for verses. The next lesson is how to <em>view</em> only those hearts. This lesson is only the tag."},{"type":"prose","html":"The same idea applies to audio effect presets. The manual notes that each effect category has a unique icon, which makes favorites easier to scan later. You can heart an effect the same way you heart a Track Preset."},{"type":"prose","html":"Hearts are not user presets. A heart still points at the factory (or user) item. If you twist knobs, you get an asterisk and you must <strong>Save</strong> to keep the tweak. Favoriting the original does not save the tweak."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the browser on a Drum Rack track.","Tap the heart next to a kit you actually like. Confirm the heart fills or stays marked.","Heart two more presets in other categories (one bass, one melodic).","Tap a heart again to unfavorite, then heart it once more so you still have three favorites.","Open an effect tab, tap the effect name, and heart one effect preset.","Close the browser. You will filter to these hearts in the next lesson."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you favorite a Track Preset? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the heart icon next to its name. Tap again to remove it.","Does a heart save knob changes? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Hearts tag the preset. An asterisk plus <strong>Save</strong> creates a user preset.","Can you favorite effects as well as instruments? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. The manual describes adding audio effect presets to Favorites."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1.1 (heart icon; Favorite filter)","Ableton Note Manual §7.4 (favorite audio effect presets)","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (search and favorite samples/presets)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Heart five presets you would actually start a sketch with. Unheart anything you hearted only to test the icon."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-7","title":"7.1.7 — Filter by Favorite","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.7 — Filter by Favorite","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open <strong>Filters</strong>, choose <strong>Favorite</strong>, and load from the short list of presets you hearted."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"After you have hearts, tap the <strong>Filters</strong> icon and select <strong>Favorite</strong>. The list becomes only those tagged presets. This is the manual’s documented path to “browse the presets you added to the Favorites list.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"The Favorites list is how you start a session in under a minute: open a track, filter Favorite, pick a kit, <strong>Done</strong>, next track. It is also how you avoid falling into whatever random Track Preset the new track offered."},{"type":"prose","html":"Because effect presets can be hearted too, a Favorites list may mix instruments and effects. Effect category icons help you tell them apart. Load an instrument favorite on an instrument tab. Load an effect favorite from an effect tab."},{"type":"prose","html":"Clear the Favorite filter when you need the rest of Core Library again. Leaving it on is the usual reason “the library disappeared.”"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the browser. Tap <strong>Filters</strong> → <strong>Favorite</strong>.","Confirm you see only the presets you hearted in the previous lesson.","Tap one, play the pads, tap <strong>Done</strong>.","Reopen the browser, keep the Favorite filter on, and load a second hearted preset on another track.","Clear the Favorite filter. Confirm Core Library categories return.","Turn Favorite back on once from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you view only hearted presets? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Filters</strong> icon, then select <strong>Favorite</strong>.","Why might Favorites show both kits and delays? <strong>Answer:</strong> You can favorite Track Presets and audio effect presets. Icons distinguish them.","What do you do if the browser looks almost empty? <strong>Answer:</strong> Check whether the Favorite filter is still enabled."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1.1 (Filters → Favorite; Favorites list)","Ableton Note Manual §7.4 (favorite effects; unique category icons)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Build one three-track sketch using only the Favorite list: drums, bass, and a melodic preset. No category browsing."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-8","title":"7.1.8 — Device-type instrument filter","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.8 — Device-type instrument filter","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Filter the browser by device type so you see only Drift, only Drum Rack, only Sampler, or only Wavetable."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.5 adds “preset filter by device type.” That filter lives with the other Filters (the icon beside search). Use it when you care about the engine, not the musical category."},{"type":"prose","html":"A “Bass” category can contain Drift basses and Sampler basses. Device type cuts across categories: every Drift, regardless of whether the factory filed it under bass or lead. That is the filter for “I want to design with Drift today” or “I need a Drum Rack, not a melodic kit-looking preset.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"The local extracts name the feature but do not print the exact filter labels. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for the on-screen wording. Open Filters on a 1.5+ install and copy the device-type names you see. Expect them to match the four instruments: Drift, Drum Rack, Melodic Sampler, Wavetable."},{"type":"prose","html":"Device type plus Favorite is a powerful pair if both can be active — not documented as a combination. Test. If they stack, you get “my favorite Drifts.” If they replace each other, pick one."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm Note is 1.5 or later (<strong>Settings → Help</strong>).","Open <strong>Filters</strong> and find the device-type options. Write the labels.","Filter to <strong>Drum Rack</strong> (or the label you see). Confirm every result is a 16-pad kit.","Filter to <strong>Drift</strong>. Confirm the first-tab icon is Drift after you load one.","Repeat for Melodic Sampler and Wavetable.","Clear the device-type filter."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which version adds filter-by-device-type? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.5.","Why use device type instead of a Core Library category? <strong>Answer:</strong> Categories mix roles. Device type keeps one engine (for example all Drift presets).","Are the exact filter labels in the extracts? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <code>{type:'gap'}</code>. Use the labels on your device."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.5 (Browser: preset filter by device type)","Ableton Note Manual §7.1 (four instruments); §7.1.1 (Filters icon)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Filter to each device type and heart one preset per type. You now have a four-engine starter palette in Favorites."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-9","title":"7.1.9 — Preview audio + pad test before Done","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.9 — Preview audio + pad test before Done","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Hear a preset two ways — the automatic preview and your own pads — before you tap <strong>Done</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In the browser, tap a preset name to trigger an <strong>audio preview</strong> of that device’s sound. Below the list, the pads stay live. Play them to test the sound in your fingers: velocity, range, whether the kit’s snare is the snare you meant."},{"type":"prose","html":"Those two listens are different. Preview is a canned snapshot. Pads are how the preset will feel in this Set’s key, tempo, and (for drums) velocity. A pretty preview can still be a stiff pad layout. A dull preview can still be the right kit once you play it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Done</strong> is the commit. Until you tap it, you are shopping. After <strong>Done</strong>, the selected preset is loaded on the track, including its two effects. If you leave the browser without <strong>Done</strong>, you keep what was already on the track — do not assume a preview replaced anything."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is the anti-random habit. New tracks arrive with a random Track Preset. Replace it only after preview + pads, not after the name sounded cool."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the browser on a track you are willing to change.","Tap a preset name and only listen to the preview. Do not play yet.","Play the pads. Notice anything the preview hid (dead velocity, wrong octave, busy FX).","Tap two more presets. For each, do preview first, pads second.","Tap <strong>Done</strong> only on the one you will keep.","Play the clip (or a new take) and confirm the loaded sound matches the pad test."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does tapping a preset name in the list do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It triggers an audio preview. It does not, by itself, finish the load.","Why play the pads before <strong>Done</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> Pads let you test the actual playable sound, not only the preview.","What does <strong>Done</strong> do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Closes the browser and loads the selected preset on the track."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1.1 Browsing Presets (preview, pads, Done)","Ableton Note Manual §7.1 (random Track Preset on tracks)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Reject five presets after the pad test even if the preview was fine. Load the sixth only if it survives both listens."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-10","title":"7.1.10 — Modified asterisk","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.10 — Modified asterisk","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Read the asterisk next to a preset name as “this Track Preset no longer matches the stored version.”"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When you tweak a preset’s parameters, Note shows an <strong>asterisk</strong> next to the name. That mark means the loaded preset is modified. The factory (or user) file on disk is not overwritten. The asterisk is a warning light: if you browse away without saving, the tweak lives only in this track of this Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"Any tabulated knob can trigger it: a Drift macro, a Drum Sampler <strong>Decay</strong>, an FX dry/wet. Double-tap reset (next module) can take the asterisk away if you return every changed control to default — the extracts do not promise the asterisk logic in that much detail. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for the exact clear condition. Watch the name as you reset."},{"type":"prose","html":"The asterisk is not a favorite and not a user preset. Heart and Save are separate. Treat * as “dirty.” Save if the sound should exist outside this track. Leave it dirty if this is a one-Set accident."},{"type":"prose","html":"Automation can also move knobs. A dot under a parameter name means automation, which is not the same as the asterisk. You can have both."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load a factory preset. Confirm the name has no asterisk.","Move one instrument knob. Confirm the asterisk appears.","Move an effect knob. Confirm the asterisk stays.","Double-tap the knobs you moved to reset them. Watch whether the asterisk clears.","Move one knob again so the name is dirty.","Do <strong>not</strong> save yet. That is the next lesson."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the asterisk mean? <strong>Answer:</strong> The loaded preset has been modified relative to the stored version.","Did you overwrite the factory preset by turning a knob? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The asterisk marks an unsaved modification on this track.","Is the asterisk the same as the automation dot? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The dot marks a parameter that has automation."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1.2 User Presets (asterisk)","Ableton Note Manual §7.5 (automation dot)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Dirty three presets on three tracks. Leave the asterisks visible. You will save one of them in the next lesson and leave the others as Set-only tweaks."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-11","title":"7.1.11 — Save user preset","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.11 — Save user preset","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Save a modified Track Preset to the User Library so you can load the same sound in another Set."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When the asterisk is showing, tap the preset name to open the browser, then tap the <strong>Save</strong> icon. That writes a <strong>user preset</strong>. The modification becomes a recallable item, not just a dirty track."},{"type":"prose","html":"Save is how a one-off bass becomes your bass. It is also how a kit you spent twenty minutes shaping survives the next + New. Cloud and Share package the Set you are in; they do not replace a User Library preset you might want in a different project."},{"type":"prose","html":"The extracts do not print the full naming dialog. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for illegal-character rules on preset names. Use a simple name you can search later."},{"type":"prose","html":"Saving a Track Preset stores the instrument plus its two effects as they currently are. If you still need to swap an effect, do it before Save. You can save again after further tweaks; whether that overwrites or creates a second item is not specified. <code>{type:'gap'}</code>. After saving, look in User Library (next lesson) and see what appeared."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Start from a dirty preset (asterisk showing) that you actually like.","Tap the preset name.","Tap the <strong>Save</strong> icon in the browser.","Give it a name you will recognize in a week.","Confirm the asterisk situation after the save (gone, or still dirty — write what you see).","Do not delete the track. You will find this preset in User Library next."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What two taps save a user preset? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the preset name, then tap the <strong>Save</strong> icon in the browser.","What does that store? <strong>Answer:</strong> The modified Track Preset (instrument + its two effects) in the User Library.","Does saving happen automatically when the asterisk appears? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Asterisk means modified. Save is a separate action."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1.2 User Presets (Save icon)","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (save user presets)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Save one user preset from the dirty tracks you made last lesson. Name it after the role (for example “Sketch Kit A”), not after the factory name."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-11</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-12","title":"7.1.12 — User Library by instrument category","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.12 — User Library by instrument category","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Find saved user presets in <strong>User Library</strong>, filed by instrument category, not in Core Library."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"User presets land in the <strong>User Library</strong>. From the browser, tap the back arrow until you reach the <strong>Libraries</strong> section, then open <strong>User Library</strong>. The manual says those presets are organized by <strong>instrument category</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is why you save a Drift bass and later look under the Drift (or bass) grouping, not under Core Library Mallets. If you cannot see a save, you are in the wrong library. Core Library is factory. User Library is yours. Current Set recordings are a third place (sample chapter), not a preset folder."},{"type":"prose","html":"Samples you save from Drum Rack or Melodic Sampler also live under User Library paths (Recordings → Drum Sampler or Melodic Sampler). Do not confuse a saved <em>sample</em> with a saved <em>Track Preset</em>. This lesson is the preset side."},{"type":"prose","html":"On disk, the Note folder also holds User Library content (<strong>Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note → User Library</strong>). Back that folder up before you delete the app. Cloud is not that backup."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the browser. Tap back until you see <strong>Libraries</strong>.","Open <strong>User Library</strong>.","Open the instrument category that should hold the preset you saved last lesson.","Tap it, preview, play pads, <strong>Done</strong>. Confirm it is the sound you saved.","Navigate back to <strong>Core Library</strong> and confirm the same name is not filed there as factory.","Repeat the path from a different track without reading this page."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you reach user presets? <strong>Answer:</strong> Browser back arrow → <strong>Libraries</strong> → <strong>User Library</strong>, then the instrument category.","Why would a just-saved bass be missing in Core Library? <strong>Answer:</strong> User presets are not stored in Core Library.","Where is User Library on the device if you need a backup? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note → User Library</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1.2 User Presets","Working with Samples in Note (User Library sample paths)","Note FAQ (Ableton Note folder; Cloud is not backup)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Cold-find your saved preset from a new track: Libraries → User Library → category → <strong>Done</strong>. Time yourself. Repeat until it is under thirty seconds."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-12</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-13","title":"7.1.13 — Templates folder (empty racks top)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.13 — Templates folder (empty racks top)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Look for empty instrument racks in the browser, and treat a factory “Templates” folder as unsourced unless you see it on your device."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The syllabus names a <strong>Templates</strong> folder with empty racks at the top. The local extracts do <strong>not</strong>. The Ableton Note Manual describes Core Library categories and User Library by instrument category. It never says “Templates.” The Ableton Note.md extract lists <strong>Set Templates</strong> as a <em>community wishlist</em> item — a desired future feature, not a shipping one."},{"type":"prose","html":"<code>{type:'gap'}</code> for an official Templates folder of empty Drift / Drum Rack / Sampler / Wavetable racks."},{"type":"prose","html":"What you <em>can</em> do with sourced tools:"},{"type":"list","items":["Load any factory Track Preset of the right device type and then replace its sample or twist its macros until it is “empty enough” for design work.","Save that starting point as a <strong>user preset</strong>.","Duplicate a Set you already configured (community tip: name one “My Template” and duplicate it). That is a Set duplicate, not a browser Templates folder."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"If your installed Note <em>does</em> show a Templates folder, copy the path and the Note version from <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. Use those empty racks. Do not claim the manual documented them."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the browser and walk <strong>Libraries → Core Library</strong>. Look for a folder named Templates.","Walk <strong>User Library</strong> the same way.","Write what you found: Templates present (version ___) or not present.","If you find empty racks at the top of a category, load one of each device type and confirm they are truly empty (no sample / init macros).","If you find nothing, load the simplest factory Drift and Drum Rack you can hear and save them as user “Init Drift” / “Init Kit” starters.","Optional: duplicate a configured Set and rename it as your working template Set."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Is a Templates folder documented in the local extracts? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <code>{type:'gap'}</code>. Set Templates appear only as a wishlist item in Ableton Note.md.","How can you still start from a known empty-ish rack? <strong>Answer:</strong> Load a simple factory preset of that device, then save it as a user preset — or duplicate a prepared Set.","What should you record if Templates exists on your phone? <strong>Answer:</strong> The folder path and the Note version."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1.1–7.1.2 (Core Library; User Library)","Ableton Note.md §6.1 wishlist (Set Templates) and §6.2 (duplicate a template Set)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Create two user presets named so you can find them cold: one init-style Drum Rack, one init-style Drift. Those are your stand-ins for empty racks."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-13</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-14","title":"7.1.14 — Content updates: Drift racks, 808s, KUČKA kits, Tamuz, etc.","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.14 — Content updates: Drift racks, 808s, KUČKA kits, Tamuz, etc.","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Know which library updates the local release notes actually name, and treat KUČKA / Tamuz / named 808 packs as gaps unless they appear in your Core Library."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Factory content grows with app updates. The syllabus asks for a survey: Drift racks, 808s, KUČKA kits, Tamuz, and similar. The condensed <strong>Note Release Notes</strong> extract does <strong>not</strong> mention KUČKA, Tamuz, or a dedicated 808 pack. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for those names."},{"type":"prose","html":"What the extract <em>does</em> name:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>1.5</strong> — full Drift device purchase/expand; device-type filter (Drift presets are first-class).","<strong>2.0.1</strong> — Demo Sets by Alice Ivy, Jose Castillo, and Heavy Mellow (audio tracks); <strong>Auto Shift</strong> and <strong>Erosion</strong> presets.","<strong>1.4</strong> — Auto Filter.","<strong>1.7.2</strong> — Auto Pan–Tremolo."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Beatmaking with Ableton Note talks about the <em>idea</em> of an 808 bass (an extended, detuned kick used as a tuned bass line). That is pedagogy, not a content-drop changelog."},{"type":"prose","html":"Open Core Library on <em>your</em> version and write the artist or pack names you actually see. App Store update notes beyond the local extract may list more; do not invent them here. If KUČKA or Tamuz kits are in your browser, they are real for your install. They are still unsourced in this folder."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Record your Note version from <strong>Settings → Help</strong>.","Open <strong>Core Library</strong> and scan category names plus any artist or pack folders.","Search for “808”, “Kucka”, “KUČKA”, and “Tamuz”. Write hit or miss.","Filter by device type and note whether Drift has a large preset set (expected from 1.5 onward).","Open Demo Sets if present and write the artist names you see (2.0.1 names three).","Keep the written survey with the Set or in a note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which named library additions are in the local 2.0.1 notes? <strong>Answer:</strong> Demo Sets by Alice Ivy, Jose Castillo, Heavy Mellow; Auto Shift and Erosion presets.","Are KUČKA kits and Tamuz documented in the extracts? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <code>{type:'gap'}</code>.","What 808 fact <em>is</em> sourced? <strong>Answer:</strong> Beatmaking with Ableton Note describes 808-as-tuned-bass as a beatmaking role, not as a named factory pack."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.4, 1.5, 1.7.2, 2.0.1","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (808/bass line)","Ableton Note Manual §7.1.1 (Core Library)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Ten-minute library walk. List five presets you did not know you had. Heart two. Ignore names you cannot find."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-14</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-1-15","title":"7.1.15 — Lab: favorite 10 presets; save 1 user","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.15 — Lab: favorite 10 presets; save 1 user","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.1 — Browser &amp; preset system</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Build a ten-heart starter palette and one saved user preset you can find cold in User Library."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This lab locks the browser skills. You leave with a Favorites list you would actually start a sketch from, plus one user preset that is <em>yours</em> — knobs moved, asterisk saved."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pick hearts across roles, not ten leads. Beatmaking with Ableton Note’s parts still apply: kick/kit, bass or 808-style bass, snare character (often inside the kit), a hat-capable kit or perc, chords/pad, a shot or lead. Device-type filter helps you grab at least one Drift, one Drum Rack, one Sampler, one Wavetable if your version has the 1.5 filter."},{"type":"prose","html":"The user preset should be a sound you tweaked for <em>this</em> week’s working title, not a random knob twist. Save after the tweak. Then navigate Libraries → User Library → instrument category and load it on a different track to prove the path."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not spend the session browsing for its own sake. Ten hearts. One save. Stop."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write a working title (one line).","Heart ten presets you would use under that title. Use categories, search, and device-type filter as needed.","Filter <strong>Favorite</strong> and confirm all ten appear.","Load one of them, change at least two knobs until it fits the title better, and confirm the asterisk.","<strong>Save</strong> it as a user preset with a name that includes the title or the role.","On a new track, load that user preset from <strong>User Library</strong> without using Favorites."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you prove the ten favorites exist? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Filters → Favorite</strong> shows them.","How do you prove the user preset exists? <strong>Answer:</strong> It loads from <strong>Libraries → User Library →</strong> its instrument category on a different track.","Why not heart the dirty factory preset instead of saving? <strong>Answer:</strong> A heart does not store knob changes. Save does."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1.1–7.1.2","Note Release Notes 1.5 (device-type filter); 1.0.12 (favorites; user presets)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (working title; drums/bass/harmony/lead)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Close Note. Reopen. Find your user preset and two favorites in under a minute. If you fail, repeat the path until it is automatic."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-1-15</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-7-2-102","code":"7.2","title":"7.2","blurb":"7.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l7-2-1","title":"7.2.1 — Drag up/down or left/right on knobs","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.1 — Drag up/down or left/right on knobs","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.2 — Parameter UI grammar (all devices)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Change any Track Preset parameter by dragging its knob vertically or horizontally."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Device parameters live in the first Track Preset tab. The same gesture works on Drift, Drum Rack, Melodic Sampler, Wavetable, and on the two effect tabs: drag <strong>up or down</strong> on a knob to change its value. You can also drag <strong>left or right</strong>. Both directions are official."},{"type":"prose","html":"You are not typing Hertz values. You are not opening a Live device view. The knob under your finger is the whole editor for that parameter on the compact surface. Which knobs appear depends on the instrument and on the individual preset (especially Drift and Wavetable)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Play a clip or hold a pad while you drag so you hear the change. A filter that does nothing until the last third of the throw is still doing its job — many presets start with the filter already open. If a knob seems dead, check for automation (a dot under the name) or try a different preset."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.0.6 removed direct-touch from <em>dials</em> for accessibility, while keeping direct-touch on pads and waveforms. You still drag knobs; the release note is about how VoiceOver/direct-touch interacts with dials, not a ban on dragging."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Drift or Wavetable track and hold a note (or play a clip).","Drag one knob up, then down. Listen.","On the same knob, drag left, then right. Confirm both axes change the value.","Repeat on a Drum Rack pad parameter and on one FX knob.","Reset nothing yet. Just notice how far you must drag to hear a musical change.","Switch presets and drag the first knob again. Confirm the control set can differ."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which drag directions change a knob? <strong>Answer:</strong> Up/down and left/right.","Do all presets show the same knobs? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Available parameters differ by instrument and, for Drift and Wavetable, by preset.","Where do those knobs live? <strong>Answer:</strong> The first Track Preset tab for the instrument; effect knobs on the effect tabs."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2 Device Parameters in Track Presets","Note Release Notes 1.0.6 (direct-touch removed from dials for accessibility)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one clip, drag only filter-related knobs until the sound goes from dull to thin and back. Stop when you can do it without looking at the value."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-2-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-2-2","title":"7.2.2 — Double-tap reset to default","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.2 — Double-tap reset to default","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.2 — Parameter UI grammar (all devices)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Double-tap a knob (or a mixer fader) to return it to the default value for this Track Preset."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Double-tap a parameter to reset it to its default. That sentence is in the device-parameter chapter and again in Note 1.0.15, which also lets you double-tap a <strong>volume fader</strong> to reset. Default means the stored value in the selected Track Preset, not a universal zero and not a frequency you invent."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the parameter is automated, <strong>Return to Default</strong> is greyed out in the parameter options menu. Double-tap will not give you a clean factory knob while a line of automation owns it. Delete the automation first (parameter menu → <strong>Delete</strong>), then reset if you still need the stored default."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use double-tap when a filter sweep went too far or you lost the preset’s identity. It is faster than reloading the whole Track Preset (which would also throw away the other knobs you still want). Reset one control. Leave the rest."},{"type":"prose","html":"Mixer <strong>M</strong> / <strong>S</strong> and sliders follow the same double-tap idea on faders from 1.0.15. This lesson’s home is still the Track Preset knobs."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load a preset. Move one knob far from where it started.","Double-tap that knob. Confirm it jumps back.","Move three knobs. Double-tap only the middle one. Confirm the other two stay.","Open the mixer and move a track fader. Double-tap it (1.0.15+). Confirm it resets.","If a knob refuses to reset, look for an automation dot and read the next lessons.","Repeat the instrument-knob double-tap from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you reset a knob to the preset default? <strong>Answer:</strong> Double-tap it.","What else can you double-tap reset from 1.0.15? <strong>Answer:</strong> Volume faders.","When is Return to Default unavailable? <strong>Answer:</strong> When that parameter is automated — the option is greyed out."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2 (double-tap reset); §7.5.1 (Return to Default greyed when automated)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (double-tap parameters or volume fader to reset)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Reset drill: move five knobs on one preset, then double-tap them home in any order. Reload the preset only if a double-tap failed for a reason you can name."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-2-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-2-3","title":"7.2.3 — Single-tap parameter options menu","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.3 — Single-tap parameter options menu","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.2 — Parameter UI grammar (all devices)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Single-tap a parameter to open its options menu: reset, plus <strong>Insert</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong> automation."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>single tap</strong> on a parameter opens the parameter options menu. From there you can reset the parameter, <strong>Insert</strong> automation, or <strong>Delete</strong> automation. Insert writes a straight line of automation at the current setting. Delete removes automation for that parameter."},{"type":"prose","html":"That menu is how you work without relying on a dotted-line capture. Transport must be running to <em>capture</em> knob moves with <strong>Add</strong>. Insert/Delete from the menu are the explicit commands. When automation exists, <strong>Return to Default</strong> is greyed out."},{"type":"prose","html":"Tap the <strong>name</strong> / the parameter (the manual says single-tap the parameter; the automation section says tap a parameter name). If a tap starts a drag instead, lift and tap more briefly. Long-drag is still value change. Short tap is the menu."},{"type":"prose","html":"Level 8 goes deep on automation. Learn the menu now so you can reset safely and recognize why a knob will not return to default."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Single-tap a knob (or its name) so the options menu opens.","Name the commands you see: reset / <strong>Return to Default</strong>, <strong>Insert</strong>, <strong>Delete</strong>.","Close the menu. Drag the knob to a new value.","Open the menu again and use reset. Confirm the value returns (if not automated).","With transport running, insert a straight automation line, play, then <strong>Delete</strong> it from the same menu.","Confirm <strong>Return to Default</strong> greys out while automation is present, then works after Delete."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does a single tap on a parameter open? <strong>Answer:</strong> The parameter options menu (reset, Insert automation, Delete automation).","What does <strong>Insert</strong> do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Adds a straight line of automation at the parameter’s current setting.","What does <strong>Delete</strong> do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Removes automation for that parameter."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2 (single-tap options menu)","Ableton Note Manual §7.5.1 Parameter Options Menu"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one clip, Insert a straight filter line, listen to the static automated value, Delete it, then double-tap reset. You just used both reset paths."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-2-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-2-4","title":"7.2.4 — Focused single-parameter mode","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.4 — Focused single-parameter mode","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.2 — Parameter UI grammar (all devices)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tap a visual control (filter, LFO, ADSR, and similar) to enter the 2.x focused single-parameter view, then leave with <strong>Done</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note <strong>2.0.1</strong> adds a focused mode: tapping a visual control — the notes list filter, LFO, ADSR, and so on — opens single-parameter access. You are no longer sharing the row with every other knob. One control takes the surface so you can see the curve or display you are dragging."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Done</strong> exits. There is no extra save step. The value you left is the value."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is version-gated. The pre-2.0 manual extract describes only drag, double-tap reset, and the options menu. If your install is 1.x, you will not get focus mode. Update, or stay on the compact knobs. Do not blend 2.x audio-track rules into this lesson; focus mode is a device-UI improvement, not a new instrument."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use focus when a filter handle or envelope is too small to drag honestly on a phone. Use the compact row when you are balancing several macros at once."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm Note is 2.0.1 or later (<strong>Settings → Help</strong>).","Open a preset that shows a visual filter, LFO, or ADSR display.","Tap that visual control. Confirm you enter a focused single-parameter view.","Drag the control. Listen.","Tap <strong>Done</strong>. Confirm you are back on the compact Track Preset tabs and the value stuck.","If you are on 1.x, write “no focus mode” and skip to dragging the same control on the compact tab."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What enters focused single-parameter mode on 2.0.1? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tapping a visual control (filter, LFO, ADSR, etc.).","How do you leave? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Done</strong>.","Does the pre-2.0 manual document this mode? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is a 2.0.1 improvement."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (tapping a visual control enters focused single-parameter access; Done exits)","Ableton Note Manual §7.2 (pre-2.0 knob gestures only)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On 2.x, open focus mode on a filter display and sweep it once through a held note. Exit with <strong>Done</strong>. Repeat on an envelope display if the preset has one."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-2-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-2-5","title":"7.2.5 — iPad side-by-side visual + dials","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.5 — iPad side-by-side visual + dials","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.2 — Parameter UI grammar (all devices)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On iPad (Note 2.0.1+), use focused mode’s side-by-side visual and dials; on iPhone, know that layout is not what you will see."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The same 2.0.1 focus mode behaves differently by device. The release notes say <strong>iPad shows visual and dials side by side</strong>. The graphic (filter shape, LFO, ADSR) and the numeric/dial control share the screen. <strong>Done</strong> still exits."},{"type":"prose","html":"iPhone is not described as side-by-side. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for the exact iPhone focused layout. Expect a stacked or single-surface view. The lesson on iPhone is recognition: you still tap the visual to focus; you just will not get the iPad split."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note runs on iPhone and iPad (iOS/iPadOS 15+). This is one of the few documented UI differences between them (another is Magic Keyboard on iPad, which is out of scope here). More pads on larger screens (1.4) is a related but separate fact."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you only have an iPhone, read this as a transfer note for the day you open the Set on an iPad. The preset is the same. The focused editor is roomier."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm device type and Note version.","On iPad 2.0.1+: tap a visual control. Confirm the visual and the dials sit side by side. Change a value. <strong>Done</strong>.","On iPhone 2.0.1+: tap the same kind of control. Write whether you see a split or a single focused control.","On 1.x: skip the tap-to-focus test and write “not available.”","Play a note while in focused view so the extra space is used for listening, not sightseeing.","Exit with <strong>Done</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does iPad show in 2.0.1 focused mode? <strong>Answer:</strong> The visual and the dials side by side.","Is the iPhone focused layout specified in the extract? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <code>{type:'gap'}</code>.","How do you exit on either device? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Done</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (iPad side-by-side visual + dials; Done)","Note FAQ (iPhone and iPad; iOS 15+)","Note Release Notes 1.4 (more pads on larger screens)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you have an iPad, shape one filter and one envelope in focused side-by-side view. If you have only an iPhone, shape the same two controls in whatever focused or compact view you have and write the difference you expect on iPad."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-2-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-2-6","title":"7.2.6 — VoiceOver support notes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.6 — VoiceOver support notes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.2 — Parameter UI grammar (all devices)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use the documented VoiceOver-related Note behaviors when you (or a student) edit parameters with VoiceOver on."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 2.0.1 adds <strong>VoiceOver support for visual controls and single-parameter access</strong>. Focused filter/LFO/ADSR views are not visual-only after that release. Earlier notes already touch VoiceOver: 1.0.6 removed direct-touch from dials for accessibility, and <strong>Capture buffer timeout is removed when VoiceOver is enabled</strong>. High Contrast (AAA) and <strong>Show Touches</strong> are separate accessibility/tutorial tools."},{"type":"prose","html":"The extracts do not provide a full VoiceOver rotor map or a list of every spoken label. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for a complete VoiceOver script. Teach what is versioned:"},{"type":"list","items":["1.0.6 — dials no longer use direct-touch (accessibility); Capture timeout off under VoiceOver.","1.0.12 — High Contrast AAA theme; touch visualization moved to Preferences.","2.0.1 — VoiceOver on visual controls and focused parameter mode."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"If you are writing tutorials, <strong>Show Touches</strong> (white circle) is the visual aid. VoiceOver is the spoken aid. Do not assume they replace each other."},{"type":"prose","html":"Turn VoiceOver on only if you know how to turn it off (iOS Side button / Home triple-click, depending on the device). This lesson is product facts, not an iOS VoiceOver tutorial."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Read your Note version. Tick which of 1.0.6 / 1.0.12 / 2.0.1 you have.","If you use VoiceOver, enable it and open a Track Preset tab. Swipe to a knob and change it.","On 2.0.1+, move to a visual control and into focused mode. Confirm VoiceOver still speaks.","Note that Capture Timeout does not apply while VoiceOver is on (1.0.6).","Turn VoiceOver off if you turned it on only for this test.","Optional: enable <strong>Show Touches</strong> for a screen recording instead of VoiceOver."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What VoiceOver improvement does 2.0.1 document? <strong>Answer:</strong> Support for visual controls and single-parameter access.","What happens to Capture Timeout when VoiceOver is enabled (1.0.6)? <strong>Answer:</strong> Capture buffer timeout is removed.","Is there a full VoiceOver control list in the extracts? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <code>{type:'gap'}</code>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (VoiceOver for visual controls and single-parameter access)","Note Release Notes 1.0.6 (direct-touch off dials; Capture timeout off with VoiceOver)","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (High Contrast; Show Touches / touch visualization)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a three-line card: your Note version, which VoiceOver items apply, and whether you will use Show Touches for teaching. Keep the card with your tutorial Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-2-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-2-7","title":"7.2.7 — Automated params show dot; Return to Default greyed when automated","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.7 — Automated params show dot; Return to Default greyed when automated","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.2 — Parameter UI grammar (all devices)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Spot the automation <strong>dot</strong> under a parameter name and understand why that knob will not reset until you delete the automation."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When a parameter has captured automation, Note puts a <strong>dot</strong> below its name. Transport running, the knob moves by itself along the stored line. The Clip Preview shows a <strong>solid</strong> line for stored automation and a <strong>dotted</strong> line for pending moves you have not <strong>Add</strong>ed yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"Because automation owns the value over time, <strong>Return to Default</strong> in the parameter menu is <strong>greyed out</strong>. A double-tap reset cannot honestly restore a single default while a line is driving the knob. Open the menu → <strong>Delete</strong> to remove that parameter’s automation. Then reset works again."},{"type":"prose","html":"Transport <strong>must</strong> be running to capture new automation from a drag. Insert from the menu writes a straight line at the current value. Those details belong to Level 8; this lesson is only the grammar: dot means automated, greyed default means delete first."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not confuse the asterisk (preset modified) with the dot (this knob is automated). You can see both."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Play a clip. Drag a filter or macro so a dotted line appears in <strong>Clip Preview</strong>. Tap <strong>Add</strong>.","Confirm the line is solid and a <strong>dot</strong> sits under the parameter name.","Single-tap the parameter. Confirm <strong>Return to Default</strong> is greyed.","Choose <strong>Delete</strong>. Confirm the dot is gone and the knob no longer moves by itself.","Double-tap reset. Confirm it works now.","Repeat once on an effect knob."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does a dot under a parameter name mean? <strong>Answer:</strong> That parameter has automation.","Why is Return to Default greyed? <strong>Answer:</strong> The parameter is automated, so it cannot restore the preset default until automation is deleted.","Dotted vs solid line in Clip Preview? <strong>Answer:</strong> Dotted = pending (not yet Add). Solid = stored automation."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.5 Automating Parameters; §7.5.1 Parameter Options Menu","Capture in Note, Automation"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Automate one knob, show someone (or yourself) the dot, delete the automation, and reset. That sequence is the whole grammar."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-2-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-7-3-103","code":"7.3","title":"7.3","blurb":"7.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l7-3-1","title":"7.3.1 — Drift is a subtractive synthesizer","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.1 — Drift is a subtractive synthesizer","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.3 — Drift deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Identify Drift by icon and definition — a subtractive synthesizer — and stay on Note’s parameter surface."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Drift</strong> is “a synthesizer based on subtractive synthesis.” That is the whole official definition in the Note manual. Subtractive here means you start with oscillator tone and shape it with filter and envelopes on Drift’s controls. Do <strong>not</strong> invent oscillator algorithms, waveforms tables, or Hertz values. Those are not in the extracts."},{"type":"prose","html":"On Note, a Drift Track Preset still follows the law of this level: instrument plus two effects (<strong>FX1</strong>, <strong>FX2</strong>). The first tab shows a <strong>subset</strong> of Drift’s parameters; the exact knobs differ per preset. Full Drift (every tab) is a purchase path documented in §7.2.1 and in Note 1.5. Later lessons cover that IAP conflict with the FAQ."},{"type":"prose","html":"Drift is not Wavetable. Wavetable is the other synth, wavetable-based oscillators, Suite-locked when the Set opens in Live Standard. Drift is the subtractive one. If the first-tab icon is Drift, you are in this module."},{"type":"prose","html":"Sound Design as a theory course can wait. Here you learn to find Drift, play it, and change the knobs Note actually shows."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Browse with the device-type filter (1.5+) or by icon until the first tab is Drift.","Play the pads. Confirm it is a pitched synth, not a 16-pad kit.","Look at the first-tab knobs. Write three labels exactly as shown. Do not add Hz.","Open <strong>FX1</strong> and <strong>FX2</strong> so you remember they are part of the Track Preset.","Load a second Drift preset. Confirm the first-tab knobs can differ.","Say the definition from memory: Drift is a subtractive synthesizer."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What kind of synthesizer is Drift? <strong>Answer:</strong> A synthesizer based on subtractive synthesis.","Does every Drift preset show the same first-tab knobs? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The default subset differs per preset.","May you quote oscillator algorithms or specific Hz values from memory of Live? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. They are not in the Note extracts."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1 (Drift definition); §7.2.1 Drift Device Parameters","Using Note Presets in Live (Wavetable lock; contrast)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Load three Drift presets in different roles (bass, pluck, pad — by ear). Play the same two-note figure on each. That is Drift’s range on Note’s surface."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-3-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-3-2","title":"7.3.2 — Default parameter subset differs per preset","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.2 — Default parameter subset differs per preset","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.3 — Drift deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Expect a different first-tab knob set on each Drift preset, and stop looking for one universal Drift panel."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By default, Drift presets contain <strong>a selection</strong> of the instrument’s parameters. “The exact available parameters differ between the individual presets.” That sentence is the lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"One bass Drift might put filter and a tone control on the first tab. A pad Drift might show longer envelope macros and an LFO amount. Both are Drift. Neither is incomplete. The first tab is the curated performance surface for that preset."},{"type":"prose","html":"After you purchase full Drift, those same default knobs still exist on the <strong>Macros</strong> tab (and they also appear in their dedicated Osc / Filter / Env / LFO / Mod / Global tabs). Padlock icons mark the macro-mapped ones. Until that purchase, the first tab <em>is</em> the instrument."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not hunt for a missing Osc tab on an unpurchased install and call the preset broken. Change presets until the knobs match the job, or buy full Drift if you need every page. Do not invent a “standard Drift layout” for the textbook."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load a Drift bass-like preset. Write every first-tab knob label.","Load a Drift pad- or lead-like preset. Write that label list.","Circle the labels that appeared in both. Circle the ones that did not.","Move only knobs that exist on the current preset. Do not look for a knob from the other list.","If full Drift is purchased, open the arrow next to the first tab and glance at <strong>Macros</strong> vs <strong>Osc</strong>. Return without changing tabs yet.","Restore a preset you actually want to play."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why don’t two Drift presets show the same knobs? <strong>Answer:</strong> Each preset exposes a different default subset of Drift’s parameters.","After a full Drift purchase, where do those default knobs live? <strong>Answer:</strong> On the <strong>Macros</strong> tab (and again in their dedicated tabs).","Is a short first tab a broken Drift? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is the preset’s curated surface."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.1 Drift Device Parameters (subset differs; Macros after purchase)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Pick one musical job (short bass stab). Try three Drift presets and keep the one whose first-tab knobs obviously serve that job. Write why the other two first tabs were the wrong surface."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-3-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-3-3","title":"7.3.3 — Purchase / restore full Drift (manual path)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.3 — Purchase / restore full Drift (manual path)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.3 — Drift deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open the documented full-Drift purchase/restore screen from the arrow next to Drift’s first tab, and know what tabs appear after a successful purchase."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This file is a duplicate filename created during rewrite. Use the official syllabus file <strong>Lesson 7.3.3 - Purchase restore full Drift (manual path).md</strong>. Content below matches that lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"The Note <strong>manual §7.2.1</strong> describes a purchase path. On a Drift track, tap the <strong>arrow next to the first tab</strong>. A screen offers to <strong>buy Drift</strong> or <strong>restore an earlier purchase</strong>. Follow the on-screen steps. Note 1.5 says the same in release-note form: access and purchase the full Drift device via the Drift icon in Device View; expand for full parameter access. Settings also gained <strong>Restore Purchases</strong> in 1.5."},{"type":"prose","html":"After a successful purchase, that arrow expands these tabs: <strong>Macros</strong>, <strong>Osc</strong>, <strong>Filter</strong>, <strong>Env</strong>, <strong>LFO</strong>, <strong>Mod</strong>, and <strong>Global</strong>. You do <strong>not</strong> have to buy Drift to finish this course. Default first-tab knobs are enough for the bass / pluck / pad labs."},{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ still says there are no in-app purchases. Teach both documents in lesson 7.3.4."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load any Drift preset.","Tap the <strong>arrow</strong> next to the first tab (or the Drift icon in Device View, per RN 1.5).","Read the screen. Write the exact buttons: buy / restore.","If you already purchased, choose restore and confirm the extra tabs appear.","If you have not purchased and do not want to, cancel. Stay on the first-tab subset.","If you purchase, open each new tab once and return to <strong>Macros</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where does the manual put the buy/restore entry? <strong>Answer:</strong> The arrow next to Drift’s first tab.","Which tabs appear after purchase? <strong>Answer:</strong> Macros, Osc, Filter, Env, LFO, Mod, Global.","Must you buy Drift to use Drift presets? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Presets already expose a default parameter subset."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.1 Drift Device Parameters","Note Release Notes 1.5 (Drift purchase/expand; Settings Restore Purchases)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Without buying anything you do not want: reach the buy/restore screen and leave it. Prefer the official <code>(manual path)</code> filename for study."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-3-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-3-4","title":"7.3.4 — Conflict check: FAQ “no IAP” vs manual purchase UI → gap if storefront","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.4 — Conflict check: FAQ “no IAP” vs manual purchase UI → gap if storefront","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.3 — Drift deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Hold two official sentences at once: the FAQ says Note has no IAPs; the manual and RN 1.5 show a Drift buy/restore UI. Record what your storefront actually presents."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Note FAQ:</strong> “Are there in-app purchases? There are currently no in-app purchases in Note.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Note Manual §7.2.1</strong> and <strong>RN 1.5:</strong> you can purchase full access to Drift (arrow next to the first tab / Drift icon), or restore an earlier purchase. Settings includes Restore Purchases."},{"type":"prose","html":"The official sources disagree by date. Do not pick a winner. The FAQ page is a general product FAQ. The manual and 1.5 notes describe a later (or storefront-specific) Drift unlock. Your App Store region may show a price, show restore only, or show nothing."},{"type":"prose","html":"<code>{type:'gap'}</code> if your device does not match either document — no buy screen <em>and</em> you expected one, or a different IAP than Drift. Write the Note version, iOS region, and the exact on-screen text. Do not invent a price."},{"type":"prose","html":"Course policy: you can complete every Drift lab on the default subset. Full Drift is optional. Never claim Note is “free plus required IAP.” The app itself is an App Store purchase, separate from Live."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Read the two quotations above out loud.","Open a Drift track and the arrow / Drift icon path from the last lesson.","Open <strong>Settings</strong> and look for <strong>Restore Purchases</strong> (1.5+).","Write one of: buy screen present / restore only / no purchase UI / other (quote it).","Write your Note version from <strong>Settings → Help</strong>.","File that note with the Set. Do not argue the FAQ away."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the FAQ say about IAPs? <strong>Answer:</strong> There are currently no in-app purchases in Note.","What do the manual and RN 1.5 say? <strong>Answer:</strong> You can buy or restore full Drift; 1.5 adds Restore Purchases in Settings.","What do you do if the storefront matches neither? <strong>Answer:</strong> <code>{type:'gap'}</code>. Record version, region, and the UI you saw."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, “Are there in-app purchases?”","Ableton Note Manual §7.2.1","Note Release Notes 1.5"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a four-line conflict card: FAQ sentence, manual sentence, RN 1.5 sentence, what <em>your</em> app shows. That card is the deliverable."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-3-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-3-5","title":"7.3.5 — Expanded tabs: Macros, Osc, Filter, Env, LFO, Mod, Global","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.5 — Expanded tabs: Macros, Osc, Filter, Env, LFO, Mod, Global","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.3 — Drift deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Name the seven full-Drift tabs in order and know that <strong>Macros</strong> is the old first-tab subset, not a second synthesizer."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"After a full Drift purchase, the arrow expands: <strong>Macros</strong>, <strong>Osc</strong>, <strong>Filter</strong>, <strong>Env</strong>, <strong>LFO</strong>, <strong>Mod</strong>, <strong>Global</strong>. That list is complete. Do not add tabs from Live’s Drift. Do not describe oscillator types or filter slopes the Note manual does not name on these pages."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Macros</strong> lists the default parameters for the selected preset — the same knobs you had before the purchase. Those parameters also exist in their dedicated tabs (oscillator controls on <strong>Osc</strong>, and so on). Padlocks mark which knobs are macro-mapped."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you never purchase, you still know this map. It is how Ableton split the instrument. The first tab you already use <em>is</em> the Macros idea."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you do purchase, click through each tab once with a note held so you hear that <strong>Filter</strong> and <strong>Env</strong> are the subtractive core, <strong>LFO</strong> and <strong>Mod</strong> move things, <strong>Global</strong> is the rest. Then come back to <strong>Macros</strong> for performance. Sound-design depth beyond the labeled knobs is out of scope for this course."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["If full Drift is unlocked, tap the arrow and open <strong>Macros</strong>, <strong>Osc</strong>, <strong>Filter</strong>, <strong>Env</strong>, <strong>LFO</strong>, <strong>Mod</strong>, <strong>Global</strong> in that order.","Write the seven names without looking.","On <strong>Macros</strong>, identify a padlocked knob. Find the same parameter in its dedicated tab.","If Drift is not purchased, write the seven names from this page and stay on the first tab. Do not invent extra pages.","Hold one note and move only a <strong>Filter</strong> or first-tab filter-like knob. Listen.","Return to the performance tab you will actually use (Macros or first tab)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["List the expanded Drift tabs. <strong>Answer:</strong> Macros, Osc, Filter, Env, LFO, Mod, Global.","What is the Macros tab? <strong>Answer:</strong> The preset’s default parameter subset — the knobs you had before buying full Drift.","Should you document oscillator algorithms here? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. They are not in the Note extracts."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.1 (expanded tabs; Macros = default subset)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Recite the seven tab names, then play a 4-bar Drift clip while touching only Macros/first-tab knobs. Full tabs are a map, not a requirement for the next lab."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-3-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-3-6","title":"7.3.6 — Macro padlock icons","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.6 — Macro padlock icons","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.3 — Drift deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Recognize the padlock in the center of a Drift knob as “this parameter is macro-mapped.”"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On full Drift, “you can recognize which parameters are assigned to macros by the <strong>padlock</strong> icon in the center of the parameter knob.” The lock is not a freeze you tap to unlock one control. It is a badge: this knob is one of the preset’s macros."},{"type":"prose","html":"Those locked knobs appear on <strong>Macros</strong> and again in their dedicated tabs. Moving a locked filter macro and moving the same filter on the <strong>Filter</strong> tab is the same parameter. The lock tells you they are tied to the preset’s performance surface."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you want the dedicated tabs only, the next lesson is <strong>Remove Macros</strong> — which removes <em>all</em> macro mappings and the Macros tab, not one padlock. There is no documented “unlock this knob only.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"Without a full Drift purchase you may still see a simple first-tab row with no padlocks. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for padlocks on the unpurchased surface. Look. Write what you see."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["With full Drift, open <strong>Macros</strong> and find a padlock in the center of a knob.","Open the dedicated tab that should own that parameter (often <strong>Filter</strong> or <strong>Env</strong>). Find the same control.","Move the macro, then glance at the dedicated tab. Confirm they stay together.","Look for a knob on a dedicated tab <em>without</em> a padlock. That one is not a macro.","Without full Drift, inspect the first tab and write padlock present/absent.","Do not tap Remove Macros yet."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the padlock mean? <strong>Answer:</strong> The parameter is assigned to a macro.","Does tapping the padlock unlock a single knob? <strong>Answer:</strong> Not documented. Removal is an all-macros action (next lesson).","Do unpurchased Drift presets document padlocks? <strong>Answer:</strong> <code>{type:'gap'}</code>. Check your first tab."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.1 (padlock icon; macros also in dedicated tabs)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Photograph or write a three-line map: macro name (as labeled) → dedicated tab where it also lives → padlock yes/no. Keep one locked macro as your performance control."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-3-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-3-7","title":"7.3.7 — Remove Macros (removes all macro mappings + Macros tab)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.7 — Remove Macros (removes all macro mappings + Macros tab)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.3 — Drift deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use <strong>Remove Macros</strong> only when you want every macro mapping gone — including the Macros tab itself."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a macro-mapped Drift parameter, tap the parameter. A screen offers <strong>Remove Macros</strong>. That action “removes all of the macro-mapped parameters, which results in the <strong>Macros</strong> tab being removed as well.” Afterward, the currently selected tab becomes the first tab in the Track Preset section."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is not “hide macros for a minute.” It is not per-knob. It is all mappings, and the Macros tab disappears. The dedicated <strong>Osc / Filter / Env / LFO / Mod / Global</strong> tabs remain (full Drift). You now perform from those pages only."},{"type":"prose","html":"Because the action is wide, do it on a <strong>duplicate</strong> of a user preset or a disposable track, not on the only copy of a sound you love. The extracts do not document an undo-restore-macros command. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for bringing Macros back other than reloading the original preset."},{"type":"prose","html":"Skip this lesson’s destructive step if you have not purchased full Drift. There is no Macros tab to remove."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm full Drift is unlocked and the <strong>Macros</strong> tab is visible.","Duplicate the track or save a user preset first.","Tap a padlocked / macro-mapped parameter so the Remove Macros screen appears.","Read the warning. Choose <strong>Remove Macros</strong> only on the disposable copy.","Confirm the Macros tab is gone and the current tab is now first.","Reload the saved original if you still want macros."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Remove Macros remove? <strong>Answer:</strong> All macro mappings, and the Macros tab.","What becomes the first Track Preset tab afterward? <strong>Answer:</strong> The tab that was selected when you removed the macros.","Is a one-knob unlock documented? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The action is all-or-nothing."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.1 (Remove Macros; Macros tab removed; selected tab becomes first)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a throwaway Drift track only: remove macros, play from Filter/Env tabs for one phrase, then reload a factory or saved preset that still has macros. Decide which surface you prefer for performance."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-3-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-3-8","title":"7.3.8 — Pressure modulation source","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.8 — Pressure modulation source","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.3 — Drift deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use the documented pressure / aftertouch facts, and treat a named Drift “Pressure” mod-source control as a gap unless it appears on your full-Drift <strong>Mod</strong> tab."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The syllabus stamps this “Pressure modulation source (1.5.2+).” The local extracts do <strong>not</strong> describe a Drift <strong>Mod</strong> tab destination labeled Pressure, and the condensed release notes have no 1.5.2 line."},{"type":"prose","html":"What <em>is</em> sourced:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Note MIDI Input:</strong> recorded types include pitch, length, velocity, and <strong>aftertouch, including polyphonic aftertouch</strong>. Not MPE, not pitch bend, not sustain.","<strong>RN 1.2.1:</strong> polyphonic aftertouch from external MIDI; <strong>Y-axis on Keys in In-Key mode</strong> for supported presets.","Full Drift has a <strong>Mod</strong> tab after purchase. The manual does not list its sources."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<code>{type:'gap'}</code> for a 1.5.2 Drift Pressure source in the extracts. If your <strong>Mod</strong> tab shows Pressure, write the version and the exact label. If it does not, use aftertouch from a class-compliant MIDI controller, or Y-axis on Keys when <strong>In Key</strong> is on and the preset supports it."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not claim MPE. Do not claim a sustain pedal. Do not invent a modulation matrix."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Check Note version. Look at full Drift <strong>Mod</strong> (if purchased) for a source named Pressure. Write present/absent.","Enable <strong>In Key</strong>, switch a Drift track to <strong>Keys</strong>, and move along the Y-axis if 1.2.1+ and the preset supports it. Listen.","If you have a class-compliant controller, play with aftertouch (including poly AT). Confirm Note records it.","Confirm pitch bend and sustain do nothing (unsupported).","If no Pressure control and no controller, write the gap and move on. Do not fake a matrix.","Play a short phrase with whatever pressure path you actually have."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which pressure-like inputs are documented? <strong>Answer:</strong> Aftertouch including polyphonic aftertouch (MIDI Input); Y-axis on Keys in In-Key mode for supported presets (1.2.1).","Is a Drift “Pressure” mod source in the local extracts? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> (syllabus 1.5.2 is not in the RN extract).","Is MPE supported? <strong>Answer:</strong> No."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note MIDI Input (aftertouch including poly AT; not MPE / pitch-bend / sustain)","Note Release Notes 1.2.1 (poly AT; Y-axis on Keys when In Key)","Ableton Note Manual §7.2.1 (Mod tab exists after purchase; no source list)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you have aftertouch or Y-axis response, capture an 8-bar Drift phrase that uses it once. If you have neither, write the gap card and skip to the bass lab."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-3-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-3-9","title":"7.3.9 — Lab: Drift bass from macros only","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.9 — Lab: Drift bass from macros only","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.3 — Drift deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Shape a usable bass on Drift using only the first-tab / <strong>Macros</strong> knobs — no invented Osc pages, no extra instruments."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Beatmaking with Ableton Note treats bass (including 808-style tuned low end) as the tuning reference for the song. On Note, a Drift bass is a Track Preset plus two effects. This lab forbids opening <strong>Osc</strong> even if you own full Drift. The default subset is the instrument."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pick a Drift preset that already sits low. Play the pads (or Keys) in the Set’s key. Move only the knobs on the first tab / Macros: typically filter, envelope, and whatever tone/drive the preset exposes. Stop when the bass locks to the kick without swallowing it. Do not type Hertz. Do not add a third effect."},{"type":"prose","html":"Save nothing yet unless the sound is already a keeper. The three-save lesson is later. Here the deliverable is one 2- or 4-bar bass clip that still sounds like the preset’s personality, just heavier or tighter."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the first tab has no obvious filter or envelope, change presets. That is the point of “subset differs.” The right bass is the preset whose macros already do bass work."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load a Drift preset that sounds low. Stay on the first tab (Macros).","Capture a 2-bar bass figure on the root and fifth (or two notes you choose).","Hold a note and drag each first-tab knob once. Keep the two or three that help.","Double-tap reset any knob that hurt the sound.","Check <strong>FX1</strong> / <strong>FX2</strong>. Lower a wet delay if it smears the kick. Do not swap devices unless the preset is unusable.","Play the bass against a drum clip. Adjust macros only until they lock."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which knobs are legal in this lab? <strong>Answer:</strong> The default first-tab / Macros knobs (plus the two existing FX if they are in the way).","Why might you switch presets instead of “finding Osc”? <strong>Answer:</strong> Each Drift preset exposes a different subset. Another preset may already be the bass surface.","What musical job does the bass have here? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sit under the drums and set the tuning reference (Beatmaking with Ableton Note)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.1 (default subset / Macros)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (808/bass line)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write the three knob labels you kept. Rebuild the same bass tomorrow from that list only."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-3-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-3-10","title":"7.3.10 — Lab: Drift pluck (short env)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.10 — Lab: Drift pluck (short env)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.3 — Drift deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn a Drift preset into a short plucked sound using only envelope-like macros on the default surface."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A pluck is a note that starts quickly and dies quickly. On Note’s Drift surface you get there with whatever <strong>Attack</strong> / decay / release / envelope macros the preset exposes — not with an invented ADSR formula. If the first tab has no envelope knobs, change presets until it does, or use full Drift <strong>Env</strong> only if you already purchased it. The lab still prefers the default subset."},{"type":"prose","html":"Play single notes, not chords. Shorten the envelope until the tone is a tick with pitch, then lengthen until it sings a little. Combined with a slightly brighter filter macro, that is a plucked bass or a picked lead. MIDI note length also matters if the sound is gated; if stretching notes in the editor does nothing, the envelope is in a trigger-style mode and the macro is the length."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not add a new delay to “make it a pluck.” If <strong>FX1</strong> is already a long reverb, dry it back so the tail does not fake a pad. The instrument envelope should do the work."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load a Drift preset that is not already a wash of pad.","Find envelope-related knobs on the first tab (Attack, decay, release, or similarly labeled macros).","Hold one pad. Shorten the envelope until the sound is a tick. Then open it until it is a pluck.","Capture a 1-bar rhythm of single notes. Adjust MIDI lengths only if the sound responds.","Dry back any effect that turns the pluck into a pad.","Play against drums. The pluck should leave space for the next kick."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What makes a Drift sound a pluck on this surface? <strong>Answer:</strong> A short envelope (the Attack/decay/release macros the preset actually shows), not a new device.","What do you do if the first tab has no envelope knobs? <strong>Answer:</strong> Switch to a Drift preset that exposes them (or use purchased <strong>Env</strong>).","Why dry the reverb? <strong>Answer:</strong> A long FX tail can hide a short envelope and fake a pad."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.1 (default subset; Env tab after purchase)","Ableton Note Manual §7.4 (FX1/FX2 on Drift)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Two versions of the same clip: very short pluck, then slightly longer. Keep the one that still leaves air for the drums."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-3-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-3-11","title":"7.3.11 — Lab: Drift pad (slow env + LFO)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.11 — Lab: Drift pad (slow env + LFO)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.3 — Drift deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Shape a Drift pad with a slower envelope and whatever LFO macro the preset shows — still no invented rates."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A pad is the opposite of the pluck lab: slow start, long sustain, motion that is not a rhythm. On the default Drift subset, that means raising Attack-like macros, lengthening decay/release-like macros, and turning up an <strong>LFO</strong> amount if the preset offers one. Full Drift’s <strong>Env</strong> and <strong>LFO</strong> tabs are legal only if purchased; they are not required."},{"type":"prose","html":"Play two- or three-note holds (In Key on if you want the pads to stay safe). Let notes overlap. If the sound still plucks, the envelope is still short. If the sound is static, look for an LFO or filter macro and move it until the tone breathes. Do not write “set LFO to 0.3 Hz.” That number is not in the extracts."},{"type":"prose","html":"Effects help a pad more than they helped the pluck. A factory <strong>Reverb</strong> or <strong>Delay</strong> on <strong>FX1</strong>/<strong>FX2</strong> can stay if it does not wash out the drums. Automation of those knobs is a Level 8 preview — one slow move is enough if you already know <strong>Add</strong>. Otherwise leave the FX static."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load a Drift preset that already feels wide, or start from the pluck preset and reverse the envelope.","Lengthen Attack-like and release-like macros while holding a chord.","If an LFO or “move” macro exists, raise it until you hear slow motion. If not, use a filter macro gently.","Capture a 4-bar held figure (clip max 16; four is enough).","Balance <strong>FX1</strong>/<strong>FX2</strong> so the pad sits behind drums and bass.","Compare to the pluck lab. They should not be interchangeable."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which controls are you looking for? <strong>Answer:</strong> Slower envelope macros and, if present, an LFO amount — plus filter if that is how this preset moves.","May you quote an LFO rate in Hertz? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Not sourced.","How long should the clip be for this lab? <strong>Answer:</strong> A few bars (4 is enough). Clips still max at 16."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.1 (Env, LFO tabs; default subset)","Ableton Note Manual §7.4–7.5 (FX; automation preview)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (harmony / linger)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Hold one chord for four bars. If you get bored at bar two, the LFO/filter is too still — move one macro until bar four still changes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-3-11</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-3-12","title":"7.3.12 — Save three Drift user presets","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.12 — Save three Drift user presets","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.3 — Drift deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Save the bass, pluck, and pad you just made as three named user presets and reload each from User Library."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Each of the three labs dirtied a Drift Track Preset (asterisk). Saving writes the instrument plus its two effects into <strong>User Library</strong>, organized by instrument category. Three names, three files, three roles."},{"type":"prose","html":"Name them so a cold search works: include the role and the working title, not only “Drift 1.” After each save, navigate Libraries → User Library and load the sound onto a <strong>different</strong> track or a new Set. If you cannot find it, you are in Core Library."},{"type":"prose","html":"Hearts are optional extras, not a substitute. A heart on the factory parent does not store your envelope."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you used full Drift tabs, those values are part of the Track Preset you save. If you only used Macros, that is enough. Do not expect the save to unlock Drift on another device — the IAP (if any) is separate from a user preset file."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Reopen the bass lab sound. Confirm the asterisk. <strong>Save</strong> as a user preset named for bass + title.","Repeat for the pluck.","Repeat for the pad.","Open <strong>Libraries → User Library</strong> and confirm all three sit under the Drift/instrument category you see.","Load each onto a blank track and play the original figure once.","Heart the three if you want them in <strong>Filters → Favorite</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does each save contain? <strong>Answer:</strong> The modified Drift Track Preset, including its two effects.","How do you prove a save worked? <strong>Answer:</strong> It loads from User Library on another track.","Does saving replace the factory preset? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It creates a user preset. The factory item remains in Core Library."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1.2 User Presets","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (save user presets)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"New Set. Load all three user Drifts. Play bass + pad together, then swap the bass for the pluck. That is a three-preset palette."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-3-12</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-7-4-104","code":"7.4","title":"7.4","blurb":"7.4","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l7-4-1","title":"7.4.1 — 16 pads; selection targets which sampler","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.1 — 16 pads; selection targets which sampler","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Select a Drum Rack pad and prove that the first-tab knobs belong only to that pad’s Drum Sampler."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The official Note Manual (§7.1) defines <strong>Drum Rack</strong> as a kit of <strong>16 pads</strong> in total. Each pad contains a <strong>Drum Sampler</strong> instrument, one audio-effect send, and one audio effect for the entire Rack."},{"type":"prose","html":"Section 7.2.2 is the rule this whole module rests on: Drum Rack presets show <strong>two pages of parameters in the first tab</strong>, and because each pad is its own Drum Sampler, <strong>those parameters affect only the sample loaded on the currently selected pad</strong>. Transpose, filter, detune, envelopes, pan, and playback FX do not rewrite the whole kit."},{"type":"prose","html":"The Ableton Note product page describes this job as finger-drumming with Drum Sampler kits. Note is not Live’s 128-note Drum Rack overview. You get 16 pads. Selection is the targeting system: tap a pad, then move a knob, and you are editing that pad only."},{"type":"prose","html":"Kit-wide sound is later: <strong>Kit FX</strong>. Pad send is <strong>Pad FX</strong>. This lesson stays on selection."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load a Drum Rack Track Preset. Confirm a <strong>4×4</strong> pad grid (Manual §8.1.3).","Tap one pad so it is selected. Play it.","Move <strong>Transpose</strong> on page 1 a few semitones. Play the selected pad, then play an unselected pad.","Confirm only the selected pad changed pitch.","Select a second pad and repeat. Recite: first-tab knobs follow the selected pad."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many pads does Note’s Drum Rack expose? <strong>Answer:</strong> 16.","What does each pad contain? <strong>Answer:</strong> A Drum Sampler, plus the Rack’s pad-send and kit-wide effect architecture.","Do first-tab parameters change the whole kit? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. They change only the selected pad’s Drum Sampler."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1 Track Presets (Drum Rack = 16 pads)","Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 Drum Rack Device Parameters","Ableton Note product page (Drum Sampler kits)","https://www.ableton.com/en/note/manual/"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a factory kit, detune only the snare pad. Play a four-on-the-floor plus backbeat. The kick and hats stay at original pitch."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-2","title":"7.4.2 — Page 1: Transpose","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.2 — Page 1: Transpose","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use <strong>Transpose</strong> on Drum Sampler page 1 to move one pad’s pitch in semitones without touching the rest of the kit."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.2 lists page-1 Drum Sampler parameters in order. The first is <strong>Transpose</strong> — it transposes the sample’s pitch up or down by semitones."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is pad-local (7.4.1). A snare that sits too low in the kit can move up a few semitones without retuning the kick. Combined with <strong>Detune</strong> (cents, next lessons) you have coarse and fine pitch."},{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ warp rule still applies on the <strong>sampler path</strong>: transposing a sample also changes playback speed because classic warp is not available on Drum Sampler / Melodic Sampler. Note 2.0.1 audio-track loops <em>are</em> warped; that is a different device. Stay on Drum Sampler here."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not invent a Hertz readout. The manual names the control <strong>Transpose</strong> and the unit as semitones."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a snare or tom pad.","Open the first Drum Sampler tab, page 1. Find <strong>Transpose</strong>.","Drag it up several semitones. Play the pad.","Drag it down below the factory value. Play again.","Double-tap the knob to reset (Manual §7.2 / RN 1.0.15).","Recite: Transpose is semitones, this pad only, sampler-path speed couples."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What unit does Transpose use? <strong>Answer:</strong> Semitones.","Does Transpose retune every pad? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Only the selected pad’s Drum Sampler.","Does sampler Transpose keep duration constant? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. FAQ: transpose also affects playback speed on the sampler path."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (Transpose)","Note FAQ (transpose couples speed; warp not on sampler path)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (audio-track warp is a different path)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Build a tom fill by transposing one tom pad to three different semitone offsets across three clips. Do not load extra samples."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-3","title":"7.4.3 — Filter Type: LP12, LP24, HP24, Peak","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.3 — Filter Type: LP12, LP24, HP24, Peak","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Switch the selected pad’s <strong>Filter Type</strong> among the four official Drum Sampler types and hear how each treats the cutoff."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.2, page 1: <strong>Filter Type</strong> switches between four filter types: <strong>low-pass 12</strong>, <strong>low-pass 24</strong>, <strong>high-pass 24</strong>, and <strong>peak</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"The manual’s own definitions:"},{"type":"list","items":["Low-pass filters remove frequencies <strong>above</strong> the cutoff.","The high-pass filter removes frequencies <strong>below</strong> the cutoff.","The peak filter <strong>emphasizes frequencies near</strong> the cutoff."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Filter types are visualized in the display on the right. That display is also a handle for cutoff and resonance or gain, depending on type (next lesson). RN 1.2 added filter-type icons on Drum Sampler."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not invent extra types (no “notch,” no Live-only EQ Eight bands). These four names are the whole list."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a bright hat or crash pad so filter changes are obvious.","Set Filter Type to <strong>LP 12</strong>, then <strong>LP 24</strong>. Sweep the handle. Hear the steeper 24 dB slope take more top end.","Switch to <strong>HP 24</strong>. Sweep until the body thins.","Switch to <strong>Peak</strong>. Sweep to hear a band of emphasis, not a shelf cut.","Return to the factory type with double-tap reset if you need the stock kit back."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Name the four Drum Sampler filter types. <strong>Answer:</strong> Low-pass 12, low-pass 24, high-pass 24, peak.","What does peak do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It emphasizes frequencies near the cutoff.","Where do you see the type? <strong>Answer:</strong> The filter display on the right, with type icons (RN 1.2)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 Filter Type","Note Release Notes 1.2 (Drum Sampler filter type icons)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Darken hats with LP 24 and thin a clap with HP 24. Play both in the same scene. Write the type you left on each pad."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-4","title":"7.4.4 — Filter display handle: cutoff + res/gain","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.4 — Filter display handle: cutoff + res/gain","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Drag the filter display handle to set cutoff and resonance or gain for the selected pad."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.2: filter types are visualized in the display on the right, “which can also be used to adjust the filter cutoff frequency and resonance or gain (depending on the filter type selected) by dragging the handle.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"So the handle is two jobs in one gesture:"},{"type":"list","items":["Horizontal / handle drag sets <strong>cutoff</strong>.","The second axis or dimension of that handle is <strong>resonance</strong> on the low-pass and high-pass types, or <strong>gain</strong> on the peak type."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 2.0.1: tapping a visual control (filter, LFO, ADSR, and so on) enters a focused single-parameter access mode. On iPad, visual and dials sit side by side. <strong>Done</strong> exits. Use that if the handle is too small."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not invent a numeric Hz label unless the device actually shows one. The source names cutoff, resonance, and gain — not a specific Hertz value."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a pad. Set Filter Type to <strong>LP 24</strong>.","Drag the handle to lower the cutoff until the pad dulls.","Drag to raise resonance until you hear a peak near the cutoff — stop before it screams.","Switch to <strong>Peak</strong>. Drag the handle and listen for emphasis (gain) instead of a low-pass dulling.","Optional on 2.0.1: tap the visual to enter focused mode, then <strong>Done</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What two filter values does the handle adjust? <strong>Answer:</strong> Cutoff, and resonance or gain depending on type.","When is the second value gain rather than resonance? <strong>Answer:</strong> When Filter Type is peak.","How do you get a larger editor on 2.0.1? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the visual control for focused single-parameter mode."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (display handle)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (focused visual-control mode)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Sweep the handle while a 1-bar hat loop plays. Capture a short automation later in Level 8; today just find a cutoff you would keep."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-5","title":"7.4.5 — Detune (cents)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.5 — Detune (cents)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use <strong>Detune</strong> to move the selected pad in cents, finer than Transpose’s semitones."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.2: <strong>Detune</strong> detunes the sample in <strong>cents</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"A cent is one hundredth of a semitone. The manual does not publish a numeric range. Use the knob as a fine tuner after <strong>Transpose</strong> has put the pad in the right octave/semitone neighborhood."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is still pad-local. Detuning a snare against a kick is how you stop two samples beating, or how you make a second snare sit slightly sharp for thickness. It is not a second Transpose."},{"type":"prose","html":"Sampler-path pitch still couples to speed (FAQ). A large detune is still a pitch-and-time change, just smaller than a semitone jump."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a snare pad. Note the factory <strong>Transpose</strong>.","Move <strong>Detune</strong> up, then down. Listen for a small pitch change, not a new note.","Play kick + snare. Adjust Detune until the snare sits with the kick.","Double-tap to reset if you lose the factory relationship.","Recite: Detune = cents; Transpose = semitones; this pad only."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What unit is Detune? <strong>Answer:</strong> Cents.","When do you reach for Detune instead of Transpose? <strong>Answer:</strong> When you need a fine pitch change, not a semitone jump.","Does Detune affect every pad? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Selected pad only."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (Detune — cents)","Note FAQ (sampler transpose/speed coupling still applies to pitch moves)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Layer two snare pads (copy comes later). For now, detune one factory snare slightly and play it against the kick. Keep the offset small enough that it still reads as one backbeat."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-6","title":"7.4.6 — Attack","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.6 — Attack","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set the selected pad’s <strong>Attack</strong> so the sample reaches peak level over a chosen time."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.2: <strong>Attack</strong> sets the time needed for the sample to reach the peak level."},{"type":"prose","html":"Short attack keeps a kick or snare transient. Longer attack fades the hit in — useful on a crash you do not want to click, or on a perc sample whose start is noisy. Attack is not Hold and not Decay. Those come next."},{"type":"prose","html":"Velocity can later modulate Attack (<code>Velocity &gt; …</code> with target Attack). Today you set the base Attack time with the knob."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not invent milliseconds. The source names the job (time to peak), not a numeric scale."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a crash or long perc pad so attack is audible.","Raise <strong>Attack</strong> until the start blooms instead of clicking.","Select a kick. Raise Attack until the punch disappears, then bring it back down.","Leave kick Attack short. Leave the crash longer if you like the swell.","Recite: Attack = time to peak level, this pad only."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Attack control? <strong>Answer:</strong> How long the sample takes to reach peak level.","Why keep kick Attack short? <strong>Answer:</strong> So the transient (the punch) is not faded away.","Is Attack the same as Hold? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Hold is how long the peak lasts after Attack (next lesson)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (Attack)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one kit, set hats to a slightly longer Attack than the kick. Play a 2-bar loop. The kick stays sharp; hats soften."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-7","title":"7.4.7 — Hold + Trig vs Gate","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.7 — Hold + Trig vs Gate","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set <strong>Hold</strong> and choose <strong>Trig</strong> versus <strong>Gate</strong> so you know whether a pad plays for a fixed time or only while it is held."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.2: <strong>Hold</strong> sets how long the sample plays at the peak level once Attack is reached. At the base of this control you tap <strong>Trig</strong> to choose between two envelope types: <strong>Trig</strong> and <strong>Gate</strong>."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Trig:</strong> the sample plays for the duration set by Hold, <strong>regardless of when the pad is released</strong>. Hold is active.","<strong>Gate:</strong> the sample plays <strong>only while the pad is held down</strong>, and <strong>Hold is deactivated</strong>."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Sample Options (§9.2) repeat the same Trigger/Gate idea for the sample itself: Trigger plays all the way through; Gate stops when the note ends. Use the page-1 Trig/Gate here as the envelope switch the manual names next to Hold."},{"type":"prose","html":"Finger-drumming closed hats often wants Gate so a lift of the finger chokes the hiss. A one-shot kick usually wants Trig so a tap still plays the body."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a long hat or crash.","Set the switch to <strong>Trig</strong>. Tap and release immediately. Confirm it still rings for the Hold time.","Lengthen Hold and tap again. The peak lasts longer.","Switch to <strong>Gate</strong>. Hold the pad down, then lift. Confirm sound stops on release and Hold is inactive.","Put a kick back on <strong>Trig</strong> so short taps still speak."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Hold do in Trig mode? <strong>Answer:</strong> It sets how long the sample stays at peak after Attack.","What happens to Hold in Gate mode? <strong>Answer:</strong> Hold is deactivated. Sound lasts only while the pad is held.","Which mode ignores how fast you lift your finger? <strong>Answer:</strong> Trig."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (Hold; Trig vs Gate)","Ableton Note Manual §9.2 Sample Options (Trigger/Gate wording)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set closed hat to Gate and open hat to Trig. Play eighths and lift early on the closed hat. The open hat still finishes its Hold."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-8","title":"7.4.8 — Decay","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.8 — Decay","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set <strong>Decay</strong> so the selected pad fades from peak to silence after Hold."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.2: <strong>Decay</strong> sets how long it takes for the sample to fade to silence from the peak level after the Hold time."},{"type":"prose","html":"Envelope order on page 1 is Attack → Hold (with Trig/Gate) → Decay. Decay is the tail. Short decay makes a clicky perc. Long decay lets a tom or snare ring into the next beat."},{"type":"prose","html":"On Melodic Sampler (module 7.5) Decay also hosts a Trig/Gate choice. On Drum Sampler, Trig/Gate lives on Hold. Do not mix those layouts."},{"type":"prose","html":"No milliseconds are published. Use your ear against the kick."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a snare or tom.","Shorten <strong>Decay</strong> until the tail is a tick.","Lengthen Decay until the ring overlaps the next kick. Pull it back.","Change Hold (Trig mode) and listen to how Decay starts <em>after</em> Hold.","Recite: Decay = fade from peak to silence after Hold."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When does Decay start? <strong>Answer:</strong> After the Hold time, from the peak level.","What does a very short Decay do to a snare? <strong>Answer:</strong> It cuts the tail so the hit is almost all transient.","Is Drum Sampler’s Trig/Gate on the Decay control? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. On Drum Sampler it is on Hold."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (Decay)","Ableton Note Manual §7.2.3 (contrast: Melodic Sampler Decay + Trig/Gate)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make hats decay shorter than the snare. Play a 2-bar loop. The backbeat still has air; hats stay tight."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-9","title":"7.4.9 — Page 2: Pan","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.9 — Page 2: Pan","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Drum Sampler page 2 and place the selected pad in the stereo field with <strong>Pan</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.2: the <strong>second page</strong> contains <strong>Pan</strong>, <strong>FX Type</strong>, <strong>Velocity &gt; …</strong>, and <strong>Vel &gt; Vol</strong>. <strong>Pan</strong> “determines the position of the pad’s sound in the stereo field.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"Page 2 is still pad-local. Panning hats a little off-center and leaving kick centered is a kit job you can do without touching Kit FX. The mixer’s track fader still moves the whole Drum Rack track. Pan here is inside the kit."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 1.2 added the second Drum Sampler parameter page and an X/Y pad for playback FX. Find Pan on that second page, not on the filter page."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not invent a numeric L/R percentage. The source names the control and the job."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On a Drum Rack, swipe or tap to <strong>page 2</strong> of the first tab.","Select the closed-hat pad. Move <strong>Pan</strong> left. Play the kit.","Select a perc pad. Pan it opposite.","Leave kick and snare near center.","Recite: page 2 Pan = this pad in the stereo field, not the track fader."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which page is Pan on? <strong>Answer:</strong> The second Drum Sampler parameter page.","Does Pan move the whole Drum Rack track? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It moves the selected pad.","What other page-2 controls sit with Pan? <strong>Answer:</strong> FX Type, Velocity &gt; …, and Vel &gt; Vol."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (second page; Pan)","Note Release Notes 1.2 (second parameter page)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Pan hats slightly right and a shaker slightly left. Export a 4-bar loop later if you want to hear it on speakers. Today, confirm the image in headphones (wired, Lesson 0.3)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-10","title":"7.4.10 — FX Type list: Stretch, Loop, Pitch Env, Punch, 8-Bit, FM, Ring Mod, Sub Osc, Noise","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.10 — FX Type list: Stretch, Loop, Pitch Env, Punch, 8-Bit, FM, Ring Mod, Sub Osc, Nois","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Step through every official <strong>FX Type</strong> on the selected pad and hear that each type has two unique parameters."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.2, page 2: <strong>FX Type</strong> sets the playback effect used to manipulate the sample. The official list is: <strong>Stretch</strong>, <strong>Loop</strong>, <strong>Pitch Env</strong>, <strong>Punch</strong>, <strong>8-Bit</strong>, <strong>FM</strong>, <strong>Ring Mod</strong>, <strong>Sub Osc</strong>, or <strong>Noise</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"“Each playback effect has two unique parameters that can be adjusted by dragging the handle in the X/Y pad on the right.” Those two parameters are the <strong>FX1</strong> and <strong>FX2</strong> targets for velocity modulation (next lessons). RN 1.2 added this second page and the X/Y pad."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not invent extra types or parameter names the screen does not show. If a factory kit already has an FX Type, that is a starting point, not a hidden eleventh mode."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a snare. Open page 2. Note the current <strong>FX Type</strong>.","Set Stretch, then Loop, then Pitch Env. Play after each change.","Continue through Punch, 8-Bit, FM, Ring Mod, Sub Osc, and Noise.","Leave the type you would actually keep on that snare.","Recite the nine names from the manual, not from memory of Live."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many FX Types does the Note manual list? <strong>Answer:</strong> Nine: Stretch, Loop, Pitch Env, Punch, 8-Bit, FM, Ring Mod, Sub Osc, Noise.","How many unique parameters does each type expose? <strong>Answer:</strong> Two, on the X/Y pad.","Which release added the second page and X/Y pad? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.2."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (FX Type list)","Note Release Notes 1.2 (second page; X/Y pad)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Put <strong>Punch</strong> on the kick and <strong>8-Bit</strong> on a perc pad. Play a 1-bar loop. If either type wrecks the kit, switch it back. Write the two types you kept."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-11","title":"7.4.11 — X/Y pad for FX1/FX2 unique params","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.11 — X/Y pad for FX1/FX2 unique params","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Drag the page-2 <strong>X/Y pad</strong> to set the two unique parameters of the current FX Type."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.2: each playback effect has two unique parameters adjusted by dragging the handle in the <strong>X/Y pad</strong> on the right. Those two dimensions are what <strong>Velocity &gt; …</strong> can target as <strong>FX 1</strong> and <strong>FX 2</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 1.2: “Added a new X/Y Pad to manipulate the playback FX with touch.” Changing Playback FX updates the views immediately (RN 1.2.1 tweak)."},{"type":"prose","html":"The X/Y pad is not the filter display (page 1). Filter cutoff/res lives on page 1. Playback-FX color lives here. Do not invent axis labels beyond what the current FX Type writes on screen."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a pad. Set <strong>FX Type</strong> to <strong>FM</strong> or <strong>Noise</strong> so the pad is obvious.","Drag the X/Y handle slowly on one axis, then the other. Play after each move.","Switch FX Type. Confirm the X/Y meaning changes with the type.","Park the handle somewhere musical, not at an extreme unless you want trash.","Recite: X/Y = the two unique params of the current FX Type (FX1 / FX2)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the X/Y pad control? <strong>Answer:</strong> The two unique parameters of the selected FX Type.","What are those two parameters called when velocity targets them? <strong>Answer:</strong> FX 1 and FX 2.","Is this the same handle as the filter display? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Filter is page 1. X/Y playback FX is page 2."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (X/Y pad; FX1/FX2)","Note Release Notes 1.2 and 1.2.1"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one perc pad, set Ring Mod and find an X/Y position that still reads as percussion. Capture a clip of four hits at that setting."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-11</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-12","title":"7.4.12 — Velocity → Filter / Attack / Hold / Decay / FX1 / FX2","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.12 — Velocity → Filter / Attack / Hold / Decay / FX1 / FX2","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Assign <strong>Velocity &gt; …</strong> to one official target and hear hard hits change that parameter."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.2: <strong>Velocity &gt; …</strong> sets how much a target parameter is modulated by note velocity. Tap the name in the lower part of the control to select the target from: <strong>Filter</strong>, <strong>Attack</strong>, <strong>Hold</strong>, <strong>Decay</strong>, <strong>FX 1</strong>, and <strong>FX 2</strong>. FX1 and FX2 are the two unique parameters of the selected playback effect."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is how a hard snare can open a filter or lengthen decay while a ghost note stays short. The amount knob is “how much.” The name underneath is “what.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"Single layout (Manual §8.1.3) gives you a large velocity surface (upper half = higher velocity). Use that when you want to hear this mapping without a MIDI keyboard."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a snare. Set <strong>Velocity &gt; …</strong> target to <strong>Decay</strong>. Raise the amount.","Play soft, then hard (or use Single layout: low vs high on the surface).","Switch the target to <strong>Filter</strong>. Repeat.","Set FX Type, then target <strong>FX 1</strong>. Confirm hard hits move the X/Y sound.","Recite the six legal targets from the manual."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Name the six Velocity &gt; … targets. <strong>Answer:</strong> Filter, Attack, Hold, Decay, FX 1, FX 2.","What are FX 1 and FX 2? <strong>Answer:</strong> The two unique parameters of the current FX Type.","How can you play a wide velocity range on-screen? <strong>Answer:</strong> Drum Rack <strong>Single</strong> layout: upper half louder, lower half quieter."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (Velocity &gt; …)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (Single layout velocity)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Map velocity to Decay on the snare. Capture a bar of ghost notes and backbeats. The backbeats should ring longer."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-12</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-13","title":"7.4.13 — Vel > Vol","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.13 — Vel > Vol","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set <strong>Vel &gt; Vol</strong> so note velocity changes how loud the selected pad is."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.2: <strong>Vel &gt; Vol</strong> sets how much note velocity modulates the sample’s volume."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is the simple dynamics control. At a low amount, hard and soft hits stay similar in level. At a high amount, ghosts are quiet and backbeats speak. It is separate from <strong>Velocity &gt; …</strong>, which aims at Filter / envelopes / FX. You can use both."},{"type":"prose","html":"Single layout (§8.1.3) is the on-screen test: upper half of the large pad = higher velocity, lower half = lower. MIDI input records velocity (Note MIDI Input KB)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not invent a 0–127 display unless the device shows one. The source names the control and the job."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select the snare. Find <strong>Vel &gt; Vol</strong> on page 2.","Raise it. Play the pad softly and firmly (or high/low on Single).","Lower it until hard and soft hits are almost the same level.","Park it where ghost notes still exist but the backbeat is louder.","Recite: Vel &gt; Vol = velocity to this pad’s volume."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Vel &gt; Vol do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It sets how much note velocity changes the sample’s volume.","Is it the same control as Velocity &gt; …? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Velocity &gt; … targets Filter, Attack, Hold, Decay, FX1, or FX2.","How do you play two velocities without a keyboard? <strong>Answer:</strong> Single layout, upper vs lower half."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (Vel &gt; Vol)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3","Note MIDI Input KB (velocity is a recorded MIDI type)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a bar of hats with mixed velocity and <strong>Vel &gt; Vol</strong> up. If every hat is the same level, the amount is too low."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-13</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-14","title":"7.4.14 — Pad FX tab + Send Amount","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.14 — Pad FX tab + Send Amount","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open <strong>Pad FX</strong> and use <strong>Send Amount</strong> so the selected pad feeds the pad-level effect more or less."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4: in Drum Rack presets the third tab is <strong>Pad FX</strong> and the fourth is <strong>Kit FX</strong>. Pad FX parameters affect <strong>individual Drum Rack pads</strong>. Kit FX applies to the <strong>entire Drum Rack</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"“The Pad FX tab always contains the <strong>Send Amount</strong> parameter, which adjusts how much of the effect is applied to the device in the currently selected pad.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"That is a send, not a replace. The pad still has its Drum Sampler. Send Amount is how much of that pad goes into the pad-level effect. Different pads can have different send amounts into the same Pad FX slot."},{"type":"prose","html":"Swap the effect by tapping its name to open the browser (same effect categories as other Track Presets). Do not invent a second send bus. The manual describes one Pad FX tab and one Send Amount."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Drum Rack. Tap the <strong>Pad FX</strong> tab.","Note the effect name. Optionally swap it (Delay or Reverb makes Send obvious).","Select the snare. Raise <strong>Send Amount</strong>. Play. Hear that pad in the effect.","Select the kick. Set Send Amount low or at default. Play the kit.","Recite: Pad FX + Send Amount = this pad into the pad-level effect."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Send Amount do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It sets how much of the selected pad goes into the Pad FX effect.","Do all pads share one Send Amount? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is per selected pad.","Which tab is kit-wide? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Kit FX</strong>, not Pad FX."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Audio Effects in Track Presets (Pad FX / Send Amount)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Send only the snare into a short Reverb or Delay on Pad FX. Keep kick dry. Play a 2-bar loop."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-14</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-15","title":"7.4.15 — Kit FX tab (whole rack)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.15 — Kit FX tab (whole rack)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use <strong>Kit FX</strong> as the effect that processes the entire Drum Rack after the pads."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4: the fourth tab on a Drum Rack is <strong>Kit FX</strong> — “an effect that applies to the entire Drum Rack.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"This is the kit glue: Saturator, Channel EQ, or Dynamics on everything that leaves the Rack. It is not Send Amount. Send Amount lives on Pad FX and is per pad. Kit FX has no per-pad bypass in the extracts. If you load Saturator here, the whole kit hits it."},{"type":"prose","html":"On Drift / Melodic Sampler / Wavetable the two effect tabs are <strong>FX1</strong> then <strong>FX2</strong> in series. Drum Rack’s split (Pad FX vs Kit FX) is special. Do not treat Kit FX as “FX2 for one pad.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"Swap Kit FX by tapping the effect name. The browser categories are the same official list (Auto Filter, Channel EQ, Chorus-Ensemble, Delay, Dynamics, Phaser-Flanger, Redux, Reverb, Saturator)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Kit FX</strong>. Read the loaded effect name.","Swap to <strong>Saturator</strong> or <strong>Channel EQ</strong> if the factory pick is too subtle.","Play the full kit. Change a Kit FX knob. Confirm kick, snare, and hats all move.","Compare with Pad FX: change Send on one pad only and confirm Kit FX still hits all pads.","Recite: Kit FX = entire Rack; Pad FX + Send = one pad."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Kit FX process? <strong>Answer:</strong> The entire Drum Rack.","Is Kit FX the same as FX2 on Drift? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Drum Rack uses Pad FX + Kit FX. Melodic devices use FX1 then FX2.","How do you change which effect is in Kit FX? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the effect name and pick from the browser."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 (Kit FX vs Pad FX; effect categories)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Put a gentle Saturator on Kit FX and a Delay send on snare only (Pad FX). That is the Drum Rack effect grammar."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-15</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-16","title":"7.4.16 — Copy/paste pads; paste on empty","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.16 — Copy/paste pads; paste on empty","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Copy a Drum Sampler pad and paste it onto another pad, including an empty one."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note Release Notes <strong>1.0.12</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["New option for <strong>Copy &amp; Pasting of Pads</strong> in Drum Sampler.","A <strong>Paste</strong> option appears next to Record/Load on <strong>empty</strong> drum pads."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the official copy workflow. You copy a designed pad (sample + pad-local parameters) and paste it to build toms, layered snares, or a second hat from the first."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 1.7.2 fixed a missing paste option on empty drum pads — if Paste is absent on an empty pad, check you are on 1.7.2 or later (<strong>Settings → Help</strong>)."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not spell the exact copy-menu label beyond “Copy &amp; Pasting of Pads” and “Paste next to Record/Load.” Use the on-screen wording. Do not invent a keyboard shortcut."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a finished snare pad. Open the pad / sample options until you see <strong>Copy</strong> (wording as shown).","Select an empty pad. Confirm <strong>Paste</strong> appears next to <strong>Record</strong> / <strong>Load</strong>.","Paste. Play both pads. Confirm the copy speaks.","Transpose the copy a few semitones so it is a tom or a second snare, not a clone on the same key.","Record the Note version if Paste was missing (pre-1.7.2 gap)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When did copy/paste pads ship? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.0.12.","Where does Paste appear on an empty pad? <strong>Answer:</strong> Next to Record/Load.","What if Paste is missing on empty pads? <strong>Answer:</strong> RN 1.7.2 fixed that. Update Note and re-check Help."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (copy/paste pads; Paste on empty)","Note Release Notes 1.7.2 (paste on empty fix)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Copy the kick to an empty pad, transpose the copy down, and play both on beat 1 as a layered kick. Save the kit as a user preset if you want to keep it (7.1.11)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-16</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-17","title":"7.4.17 — Second parameter page + X/Y","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.17 — Second parameter page + X/Y","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Treat Drum Sampler as a <strong>two-page</strong> device: envelopes and filter on page 1, pan / FX Type / velocity / X/Y on page 2."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This lesson locks the map you have been walking:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Page 1 (Manual §7.2.2):</strong> Transpose, Filter Type + display handle, Detune, Attack, Hold + Trig/Gate, Decay."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Page 2 (same section; RN 1.2):</strong> Pan, FX Type (nine playback effects), X/Y pad for the two unique FX parameters, Velocity &gt; … (six targets), Vel &gt; Vol."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 1.2: “Added a second parameter page to access new playback effects” and “Added a new X/Y Pad to manipulate the playback FX with touch.” RN 1.2.1: changing Playback FX updates the views immediately."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you only ever stay on page 1, you never see Punch, 8-Bit, or velocity-to-FX. Flip the page on purpose."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On one pad, speak page 1’s controls while you point at them.","Go to page 2. Speak Pan, FX Type, X/Y, Velocity &gt;, Vel &gt; Vol.","Change FX Type and immediately move the X/Y handle (1.2.1 view update).","Return to page 1 and confirm filter/envelopes are still there.","Recite the two-page map from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which release added page 2 and the X/Y pad? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.2.","Name three page-2 controls. <strong>Answer:</strong> Any of: Pan, FX Type, X/Y, Velocity &gt; …, Vel &gt; Vol.","Where is Trig/Gate on Drum Sampler? <strong>Answer:</strong> Page 1, on Hold — not on page 2."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (both pages)","Note Release Notes 1.2 / 1.2.1"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write the two-page map on paper. Close Note. Rebuild one pad from the paper: filter on page 1, Punch + X/Y on page 2."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-17</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-18","title":"7.4.18 — Design kick, snare, hat, perc with different FX Types","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.18 — Design kick, snare, hat, perc with different FX Types","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Give four kit roles four different official FX Types so the rack is a designed kit, not four copies of one page-2 setting."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Beatmaking with Ableton Note names the roles: kick (downbeat pulse), snare (backbeat), hats (divisions), and extra excitement (shots / perc). The Note Manual gives you nine legal FX Types to differentiate those roles without leaving Drum Sampler."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use only types from §7.2.2. A typical sourced pairing — not a law, an example:"},{"type":"list","items":["Kick: <strong>Punch</strong> or leave closer to factory if Punch wrecks the weight.","Snare: <strong>Stretch</strong> or <strong>Pitch Env</strong> for a little motion.","Hat: often cleaner with less FX; <strong>Noise</strong> only if you want trash.","Perc: <strong>8-Bit</strong>, <strong>FM</strong>, or <strong>Ring Mod</strong> so it does not sound like a second snare."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Each type still has two X/Y parameters. Selection still targets one pad. Kit FX can glue after."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Identify kick, snare, closed hat, and one perc pad.","Assign a <strong>different</strong> FX Type to each. Play after every assignment.","Adjust each pad’s X/Y so the type is audible but the role still reads.","Play a 2-bar pattern: kick on 1/3, snare on 2/4, hats eighths, perc as a shot.","If two pads still sound identical, change type, not just X/Y."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Must every pad use an FX Type? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The list is available. Factory “none-like” settings can stay if they already work.","What is illegal here? <strong>Answer:</strong> Inventing an FX Type that is not in the manual list.","Where do role names (kick/snare/hat) come from? <strong>Answer:</strong> Beatmaking with Ableton Note, plus your ears on the kit."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (FX Type list)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (kick, snare, hat, shots)","Ableton Note Manual §7.4 (Kit FX after pad design)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Save the designed kit as a user preset (7.1.11). Name it with the Set’s working title, not “Kit 1.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-18</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-4-19","title":"7.4.19 — Velocity-sensitive snare via Single layout + Vel > Vol","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.19 — Velocity-sensitive snare via Single layout + Vel > Vol","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.4 — Drum Rack &amp; Drum Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play one snare pad on the <strong>Single</strong> layout so <strong>Vel &gt; Vol</strong> (and optional Velocity &gt; Decay) makes ghosts and backbeats."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §8.1.3: <strong>Single</strong> replaces the 4×4 grid with one large velocity-sensitive surface. Select a pad in Pads layout, then switch to Single. Taps on the <strong>upper half</strong> create higher-velocity notes; the <strong>lower half</strong> creates lower-velocity notes. Single exists only for Drum Racks."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pair that surface with page-2 <strong>Vel &gt; Vol</strong> (§7.2.2) so velocity actually changes loudness. Optionally set <strong>Velocity &gt; …</strong> to <strong>Decay</strong> so loud snares ring longer (7.4.12)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Capture records velocity (Capture in Note; MIDI Input KB). Ghost notes are a musical result of these official controls, not a separate “ghost mode.”"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select the snare in <strong>Pads</strong> layout. Switch to <strong>Single</strong>.","Raise <strong>Vel &gt; Vol</strong>. Tap the lower half, then the upper half. Confirm a level difference.","Optional: Velocity &gt; Decay up. Confirm loud hits last longer.","Capture a 2-bar snare part: ghosts on the “e” and “a,” backbeats on 2 and 4.","Switch back to 4×4. The clip still plays the velocities you captured."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you open Single? <strong>Answer:</strong> Select a pad, then switch the performance layout to Single.","Which half of Single is louder? <strong>Answer:</strong> The upper half.","Which page-2 control turns that into volume? <strong>Answer:</strong> Vel &gt; Vol."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (Single layout)","Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (Vel &gt; Vol; Velocity &gt; Decay)","Capture in Note; Note MIDI Input (velocity is captured)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Keep this snare clip. You will drop it into a scene in Level 12 labs. Do not flatten velocities in the MIDI Editor yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-4-19</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-7-5-105","code":"7.5","title":"7.5","blurb":"7.5","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l7-5-1","title":"7.5.1 — Filter Freq + type LP/BP/HP","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.1 — Filter Freq + type LP/BP/HP","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.5 — Melodic Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set Melodic Sampler’s <strong>Filter Freq</strong> and switch its type among <strong>LP</strong>, <strong>BP</strong>, and <strong>HP</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.3: <strong>Filter Freq</strong> sets the cutoff for the selected filter type. Tap the type name on the lower part of the control to choose <strong>LP</strong> (low-pass), <strong>BP</strong> (band-pass), or <strong>HP</strong> (high-pass)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Official definitions in that section:"},{"type":"list","items":["Low-pass removes frequencies <strong>above</strong> the cutoff.","Band-pass allows frequencies <strong>near</strong> the cutoff and removes energy on both sides.","High-pass removes frequencies <strong>below</strong> the cutoff."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"This is a three-type list, not Drum Sampler’s four-type list (LP12, LP24, HP24, Peak). Do not paste Drum Rack filter names onto Melodic Sampler."},{"type":"prose","html":"Melodic Sampler “repitches a sample across the pads” (§7.1). The filter sits on that pitched instrument, not on one drum pad."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load a Melodic Sampler Track Preset (or an empty Sampler + a sample).","Find <strong>Filter Freq</strong>. Switch the type through <strong>LP</strong>, <strong>BP</strong>, <strong>HP</strong>.","Sweep Filter Freq on each type. Play a held note.","Park on LP or HP for a usable instrument, unless you want BP as a special color.","Recite: Melodic Sampler filter types are LP, BP, HP only."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Name Melodic Sampler’s three filter types. <strong>Answer:</strong> LP, BP, HP.","How is that different from Drum Sampler? <strong>Answer:</strong> Drum Sampler lists LP12, LP24, HP24, and Peak.","What does Filter Freq set? <strong>Answer:</strong> The cutoff for the selected type."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.3 Melodic Sampler Device Parameters","Ableton Note Manual §7.1 (Melodic Sampler definition)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Sweep LP down on a chord stab until it becomes a muted stab. Leave it there for 7.5.9."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-5-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-5-2","title":"7.5.2 — Transpose (Melodic Sampler)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.2 — Transpose (Melodic Sampler)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.5 — Melodic Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use Melodic Sampler <strong>Transpose</strong> to move the whole instrument in semitones, then remember the FAQ speed-coupling rule."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.3: <strong>Transpose</strong> transposes the sample’s pitch up or down by semitones."},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §9: Sampler plays audio at original pitch on the <strong>C3</strong> pad; other pads transpose the sample across the scale. The Transpose knob is an offset on top of that pad mapping."},{"type":"prose","html":"FAQ (Making Music with Note): on Sampler or Drum Rack, <strong>Transpose also affects the speed of playback</strong>. “Since Warp is not available in Note, it's not currently possible to adjust timing and pitch independently.” That FAQ line is the <strong>sampler path</strong>. Note 2.0.1 audio-track loops <strong>are</strong> warped (RN). Do not apply audio-track warp to this knob."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 1.5 fixed display of Transpose and Detune dials on Melodic Sampler — if a dial looks wrong, check version."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Play the C3-area pad (root). Note the sample’s original pace.","Raise <strong>Transpose</strong> an octave (12 semitones if the control lets you hear it). Listen for a shorter, brighter sample.","Lower Transpose below default. Listen for a longer, darker sample.","Double-tap to reset.","Recite: sampler Transpose = pitch and speed together."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is original pitch on Melodic Sampler? <strong>Answer:</strong> The C3 pad plays the file at original pitch (Manual §9).","Does sampler Transpose keep length constant? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. FAQ: speed changes with transpose. No independent warp on this path.","Which path <em>can</em> change tempo without pitch? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 2.0.1 <strong>audio tracks</strong>, not Melodic Sampler."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.3; §9 (C3 original pitch)","Note FAQ (transpose couples speed)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (audio-track warp contrast); 1.5 (dial display fix)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Transpose a vocal one-shot up a fifth and listen to the chipmunk shortening. That is the sampler law, not a bug."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-5-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-5-3","title":"7.5.3 — Detune (Melodic Sampler)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.3 — Detune (Melodic Sampler)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.5 — Melodic Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use Melodic Sampler <strong>Detune</strong> (cents) as fine pitch after Transpose has done the semitone work."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.3: <strong>Detune</strong> detunes the sample in cents."},{"type":"prose","html":"Same unit as Drum Sampler Detune, different instrument: here it moves the whole Melodic Sampler, because there is one sample mapped across pads, not 16 independent Drum Samplers."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use Detune to sit a sampled chord under a Drift bass, or to thicken two Sampler tracks against each other. Large detune still rides the FAQ speed coupling, just in smaller steps than Transpose."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 1.5: “Melodic Sampler: Fixed display of Transpose and Detune dials.” If Detune looks wrong, update Note."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Play a held note on Melodic Sampler.","Move <strong>Detune</strong> up, then down. Listen for a small pitch change.","Play the same note against a Drift or another track in key.","Trim Detune until beats between the two sounds slow down or stop.","Recite: Detune = cents on the whole Sampler instrument."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What unit is Detune? <strong>Answer:</strong> Cents.","Does it retune one pad only? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Melodic Sampler is one sample across the pad map.","Which RN fixed these dials? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.5."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.3 (Detune)","Note Release Notes 1.5 (Transpose/Detune display)","Note FAQ (pitch/speed coupling still applies)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Detune a pad sample slightly sharp against a Drift chord. Keep it musical, not a chorus pedal."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-5-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-5-4","title":"7.5.4 — Attack (Melodic Sampler)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.4 — Attack (Melodic Sampler)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.5 — Melodic Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set Melodic Sampler <strong>Attack</strong> so the sample reaches peak over time — pads swell, plucks stay sharp."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.3: <strong>Attack</strong> sets the time needed for the sample to reach the peak level."},{"type":"prose","html":"On a piano or guitar one-shot, short Attack keeps the transient. On a texture or vocal, longer Attack hides the room noise at the file start and turns the sound into a pad. Combined with <strong>Release</strong> (7.5.6) you shape the instrument’s edges."},{"type":"prose","html":"This Attack is instrument-wide, not per drum pad. RN 1.5.2 restored Trigger/Gate on Melodic Sampler’s parameter layout when it went missing — envelopes belong on this panel."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load a Melodic Sampler with a one-shot that has a clicky start.","Raise <strong>Attack</strong> until the click fades into a swell.","Drop Attack to minimum and play a fast line. Confirm notes speak immediately.","Leave Attack short for a stab, or long for a pad you will use in 7.5.10.","Recite: Attack = time to peak on the whole Sampler."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Attack control? <strong>Answer:</strong> Time for the sample to reach peak level.","Is it per pad like Drum Sampler Attack? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Melodic Sampler has one sample engine across the pads.","What later control fades the note after you lift? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Release</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.3 (Attack)","Note Release Notes 1.5.2 (Trigger/Gate layout on Melodic Sampler)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make two clips: same sample, short Attack (stab) vs long Attack (swell). Launch them as two scenes later."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-5-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-5-5","title":"7.5.5 — Decay + Trig/Gate (Melodic Sampler)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.5 — Decay + Trig/Gate (Melodic Sampler)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.5 — Melodic Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set <strong>Decay</strong> and choose <strong>Trig</strong> versus <strong>Gate</strong> on Melodic Sampler’s Decay control."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.3: <strong>Decay</strong> sets how long it takes the sample to fade to silence from the peak level. You tap the name on the lower part of this control to choose <strong>Trig</strong> or <strong>Gate</strong>."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Trigger:</strong> the sample plays for the duration set by Decay, <strong>regardless of when the pad is released</strong>.","<strong>Gate:</strong> the sample plays <strong>only while the pad is held down</strong>."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"This is the Melodic Sampler layout. Drum Sampler puts Trig/Gate on <strong>Hold</strong>, not on Decay. Do not hunt for a Hold knob here."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 1.5.2: “Melodic Sampler: Trigger/Gate was missing from the parameter layout” — then restored. If you cannot see the switch, check <strong>Help</strong> for 1.5.2+."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Find Decay. Switch to <strong>Trig</strong>. Tap a pad and lift immediately. Confirm the decay still runs.","Lengthen Decay. Tap again. The tail lasts longer.","Switch to <strong>Gate</strong>. Hold a pad, then lift. Confirm the sound stops with your finger.","For a pad texture, Gate + long Release (next lesson) is the usual pair. For a one-shot stab, Trig + short Decay.","Recite the two modes in the manual’s words."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is Trig/Gate on Melodic Sampler? <strong>Answer:</strong> On the Decay control.","What does Gate do? <strong>Answer:</strong> The sample plays only while the pad is held.","What if the switch is missing? <strong>Answer:</strong> Update past Note 1.5.2. The RN says it was missing, then fixed."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.3 (Decay; Trigger/Gate)","Note Release Notes 1.5.2"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play the same five-note figure in Trig and in Gate. In Gate, lift early on the last note so the phrase breathes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-5-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-5-6","title":"7.5.6 — Release (Melodic Sampler)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.6 — Release (Melodic Sampler)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.5 — Melodic Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set <strong>Release</strong> so the sample becomes silent after you lift the pad."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.3: <strong>Release</strong> sets how quickly the sample becomes silent after the pad is released."},{"type":"prose","html":"Release is the lift tail. It matters most in <strong>Gate</strong> mode, because Gate already stops the sustain when you lift — Release is the fade of that stop. In <strong>Trig</strong> mode the note may already be decaying on its own; Release is still the official “after release” control."},{"type":"prose","html":"Drum Sampler page 1 has no Release in the manual list (Attack, Hold, Decay). Do not look for a Drum-style Hold here."},{"type":"prose","html":"No milliseconds are published. Use your ear against the next note."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set Decay to <strong>Gate</strong>. Play a held chord.","Raise <strong>Release</strong> so lifting the pads leaves a tail.","Shorten Release so the chord cuts off cleanly.","Switch Decay to <strong>Trig</strong> and play stabs. Notice Release is a smaller part of the story.","Recite: Release = how fast the sample goes silent after you lift."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Release control? <strong>Answer:</strong> How quickly the sample becomes silent after the pad is released.","Which envelope mode makes Release most obvious? <strong>Answer:</strong> Gate — the note is waiting on your finger.","Does Drum Sampler list Release on page 1? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That list is Attack, Hold, Decay."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.3 (Release)","Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 (Drum contrast)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make a pad with long Release and a stab with short Release from the same sample (two clips). That is arrangement by envelope, not by a second sample."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-5-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-5-7","title":"7.5.7 — Filter LFO amount","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.7 — Filter LFO amount","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.5 — Melodic Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Raise <strong>Filter LFO</strong> so the LFO moves Melodic Sampler’s filter cutoff."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.3: <strong>Filter LFO</strong> sets how much the LFO modulates the filter cutoff."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the entire official description. The extract does not publish LFO rate, waveform names, or Hertz. {type:'gap'} Do not invent a Rate knob if the screen does not show one. Teach the named control: amount of LFO into the filter."},{"type":"prose","html":"Together with Filter Freq + LP/BP/HP (7.5.1) this is motion on a static sample — the difference between a frozen loop and a pad that breathes. Automation (Level 8) can still move Filter Freq by hand; Filter LFO is the built-in cyclic move."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set filter type to <strong>LP</strong>. Park Filter Freq mid-way so there is room to move.","Raise <strong>Filter LFO</strong> from zero until you hear the cutoff pulse.","Play a held note. Confirm the movement continues without you touching Filter Freq.","Return Filter LFO to a small amount so the pad is alive, not seasick.","Recite: Filter LFO = how much the LFO moves cutoff. Rate/shape = only what the screen shows."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Filter LFO do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It sets how much the LFO modulates filter cutoff.","May you quote an LFO rate from Live’s Simpler? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is not in the Note extracts.","What must be open for the LFO to have something to move? <strong>Answer:</strong> A filter (LP/BP/HP) with Filter Freq not already at an extreme."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.3 (Filter LFO)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Record a 4-bar pad clip with a small Filter LFO. Listen back. If you cannot hear motion, raise the amount or lower Filter Freq into the LFO’s range."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-5-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-5-8","title":"7.5.8 — Transpose couples speed without classic warp on the sampler path","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.8 — Transpose couples speed without classic warp on the sampler path","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.5 — Melodic Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"State the official conflict: Sampler/Drum Rack transpose changes speed; Note 2.0.1 audio tracks warp tempo and pitch independently."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note FAQ, Making Music with Note:"},{"type":"prose","html":"“I'm having trouble getting my imported loop/sample to play in time/key — how do I do this?”"},{"type":"prose","html":"“In Sampler or Drum Rack device view, tap <strong>Transpose</strong> to re-pitch a sample. Transposing also affects the speed of playback. Since Warp is not available in Note, it's not currently possible to adjust timing and pitch independently.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"That paragraph is still true for <strong>Melodic Sampler and Drum Sampler</strong>. It is the reason a vocal loop pitched to the Set key also shortens or lengthens."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note Release Notes <strong>2.0.1</strong> (Audio Tracks): “Audio loops are warped, so they adapt their tempo without changing the pitch and vice versa.” Compatibility/FAQ also say Link Audio arrives with Note 2.0. The FAQ “Warping is not currently available” line is older than that RN. <strong>Teach by path:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Sampler / Drum Rack = FAQ (coupled).","Audio track (2.0+) = RN (independent)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not blend them into one false rule."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Import or record a loop into <strong>Melodic Sampler</strong>. Play C3 (original).","Transpose to match the Set key. Listen: pace changes.","If you are on Note 2.0.1+, add an <strong>Audio</strong> track (RN add-track buttons) and load the same file there. Change clip tempo/transpose on the audio clip and compare.","Write two lines in your notes: “Sampler = coupled.” “Audio track 2.x = warped.”","Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong> so you know which world you are in."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What happens when you Transpose a Sampler loop? <strong>Answer:</strong> Pitch and speed both change (FAQ).","Where <em>can</em> tempo and pitch move separately? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 2.0.1 audio-track loops (RN).","Is the FAQ “no warp” line a lie on 2.x? <strong>Answer:</strong> It is still the sampler-path rule. Audio tracks are the exception. Version-gate."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ (Making Music with Note — transpose/speed; “Can I warp audio?”)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (warped audio loops)","Note Compatibility KB (Link Audio as of Note 2.0 / Live 12.4)","Ableton Note Manual §7.2.3 / §9"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Keep one coupled Sampler clip and, if you have 2.x, one warped audio clip of the same file. Label the clips in your process notes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-5-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-5-9","title":"7.5.9 — Chord stab from an imported sample","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.9 — Chord stab from an imported sample","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.5 — Melodic Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Import a short stab, map it on Melodic Sampler, and play a chord that stays in the Set’s key."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Working with Samples in Note: import <strong>WAV, AIFF, M4A, MP3</strong> (M4A/MP3 convert to WAV) via AirDrop or Share. Load into Sampler with <strong>Load</strong>. Manual §9: original pitch on <strong>C3</strong>; other pads transpose."},{"type":"prose","html":"Set <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> first (Manual §5.3). <strong>In Key</strong> snaps melodic pads to the scale. Play a three- or four-note stab voicing on the pads. Beatmaking with Ableton Note: find 3–4 chords, loop them, add delay/reverb to linger."},{"type":"prose","html":"FAQ speed coupling still applies if you Transpose the whole instrument to fit the key. Prefer choosing pads in scale over huge Transpose when you can."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep the file under <strong>100 MB</strong> (RN 1.0.6) or the browser will refuse it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Import a short chord or one-shot (WAV/AIFF/M4A/MP3). Load it into Melodic Sampler.","Enable <strong>In Key</strong> and pick a root + scale in Set Settings.","Play C3, then build a 3-note voicing on in-key pads.","Shorten Attack and Decay (Trig) so it reads as a stab, not a pad.","Optional: put Delay or Reverb on <strong>FX1/FX2</strong> (Manual §7.4)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which formats import? <strong>Answer:</strong> WAV, AIFF, M4A, MP3 (lossy types become WAV).","Which pad is original pitch? <strong>Answer:</strong> C3.","What hard limit blocks a giant file? <strong>Answer:</strong> Samples over 100 MB cannot be imported or selected (RN 1.0.6)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Working with Samples in Note (import; Load)","Ableton Note Manual §9 (C3); §5.3 (In Key); §7.4 (FX1/FX2)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (3–4 chords)","Note Release Notes 1.0.6 (100 MB)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a 2-bar stab pattern. That clip is a harmony track for a later lab. Do not mix yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-5-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-5-10","title":"7.5.10 — Reverse a one-shot into a melodic texture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.10 — Reverse a one-shot into a melodic texture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.5 — Melodic Sampler deep</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Reverse a Melodic Sampler sample and play it as a rising texture, not a one-shot attack."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note Release Notes <strong>1.0.12</strong>: <strong>Reverse Sample</strong> is an official sample option. RN 1.4.3 changed reversed-sample naming from <code>_rev.wav</code> to <code>' R'.wav</code>."},{"type":"prose","html":"A reversed one-shot puts the tail first and the transient last. Long <strong>Attack</strong> is usually wrong here — the swell is already in the file. Use <strong>Gate</strong> + <strong>Release</strong> so you can hold the reverse and cut it. Filter LFO (7.5.7) can add motion."},{"type":"prose","html":"Product page: record up to 60 seconds into Note’s samplers, then cut, filter, repitch, or add effects. Reverse is the RN 1.0.12 version of that “transform” job."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not give a click-by-click reverse menu path beyond “Reverse Sample” in sample options. Use the on-screen <strong>Sample Options</strong> wording (Manual §9.2)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load a one-shot with a clear transient (snare-like vocal, piano, guitar).","Open Sample Options. Enable <strong>Reverse</strong> (label as shown).","Play C3. Confirm the transient now arrives at the end.","Set Decay to <strong>Gate</strong>. Hold a pad to the swell, lift before the click if you do not want it.","Optional: light Reverb on FX1. Capture a 2-bar rising figure."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which RN added Reverse? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.0.12.","How did reversed filenames change? <strong>Answer:</strong> From <code>_rev.wav</code> to <code>' R'.wav</code> (RN 1.4.3).","Why use Gate on a reverse? <strong>Answer:</strong> So you can hold the swell and release before the original transient."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (Reverse Sample)","Note Release Notes 1.4.3 (naming)","Ableton Note Manual §9.2 Sample Options","Ableton Note product page (transform sampled sounds)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use the reverse clip as a scene-change riser: Scene 1 dry stab (7.5.9), Scene 2 reverse into the next downbeat."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-5-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-7-6-106","code":"7.6","title":"7.6","blurb":"7.6","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l7-6-1","title":"7.6.1 — Wavetable icon and preset browsing","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.6.1 — Wavetable icon and preset browsing","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.6 — Wavetable deep (Note surface)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Recognize the <strong>Wavetable</strong> icon, browse only Wavetable presets, and refuse to treat it as Drift."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.1: <strong>Wavetable</strong> is “a synthesizer that uses wavetable-based oscillators,” marked with its own icon in the first tab. Drift is subtractive. Drum Rack is 16 pads. Melodic Sampler repitches a sample. If the icon is Wavetable, you are in this module."},{"type":"prose","html":"Browse: tap the preset name, stay in the category, preview by tapping names, play pads, tap <strong>Done</strong> (§7.1.1). Filters and Favorites still apply. RN 1.5 added a preset filter by <strong>device type</strong> — use it so Drift racks do not hide in the list."},{"type":"prose","html":"Using Note Presets in Live: when this Set opens in Live <strong>Lite / Intro / Standard</strong>, a Wavetable-based preset is a <strong>locked rack</strong>. Macros you see in Note remain; the full Suite Wavetable device view does not. Do not teach Suite-only oscillator pages as if this studio owns them."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the browser. Filter by device type (1.5+) or hunt the Wavetable icon.","Preview two Wavetable presets. Play the same two-note figure on each.","Confirm FX1 and FX2 are still there (Track Preset law).","Recite: Wavetable = wavetable oscillators; locked in Live Standard.","Write the Note version from <strong>Help</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is Wavetable, in the Note manual’s sentence? <strong>Answer:</strong> A synthesizer that uses wavetable-based oscillators.","What happens to that preset in Live Standard? <strong>Answer:</strong> It functions as a locked rack (Using Note Presets in Live).","How do you avoid loading Drift by mistake? <strong>Answer:</strong> Device-type filter (RN 1.5) and the first-tab icon."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1 / §7.1.1","Using Note Presets in Live (locked rack)","Note Release Notes 1.5 (device-type filter)","https://help.ableton.com/hc/en-us/articles/5751167501340-Using-Note-Presets-in-Live"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Favorite three Wavetable presets that already sound like lead, bass, and pad. You will use them in 7.6.4–7.6.6."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-6-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-6-2","title":"7.6.2 — Parameters differ per preset (no false universal panel)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.6.2 — Parameters differ per preset (no false universal panel)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.6 — Wavetable deep (Note surface)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Treat the first-tab knobs as <strong>that preset’s</strong> macros, not a universal Wavetable panel."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.2.4: “In tracks containing Wavetable presets, device parameters are contained in the first tab. <strong>The exact available parameters differ between the individual presets.</strong>”"},{"type":"prose","html":"That sentence is the whole lesson. Do not invent Osc 1/Osc 2/Warp/Position as always-visible Note knobs. Some presets show a subset. The next preset may show a different subset. Full Wavetable lives in Live Suite; Note shows what the Track Preset author mapped."},{"type":"prose","html":"If a knob has a padlock, think Drift-style macros (Manual §7.2.1). Wavetable presets are still “instrument + two effects.” Change sound with the knobs you actually see (7.6.3)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load Wavetable preset A. Write every first-tab knob label.","Load Wavetable preset B. Write its labels.","Circle the labels that do <strong>not</strong> match. That is the point.","Play both. Do not hunt for a hidden “true” panel.","Recite §7.2.4: parameters differ between individual presets."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Do all Wavetable presets show the same first-tab knobs? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The manual says they differ.","May you teach Live Suite’s full Wavetable page as Note’s UI? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Not owned here; locked rack in Standard (KB).","What stays constant? <strong>Answer:</strong> Icon, two effect slots, and the Track Preset browser."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.2.4 Wavetable Device Parameters","Using Note Presets in Live"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Photograph or copy the two label lists into your process notes. Future-you will not remember which preset hid which knob."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-6-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-6-3","title":"7.6.3 — Change sound with macros first","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.6.3 — Change sound with macros first","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.6 — Wavetable deep (Note surface)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Change a Wavetable sound only with the first-tab knobs (and FX1/FX2) before you think about Live."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note’s law for every Track Preset (Manual §7.1): an instrument plus <strong>two audio effects</strong>. Wavetable’s first tab is the playable surface (§7.2.4). Drag knobs; double-tap resets (§7.2). Automation (Level 8) can capture those same knobs if transport is running."},{"type":"prose","html":"Using Note Presets in Live: those visible controls remain usable in Live Standard as a <strong>locked rack</strong>. So macros-first is not a beginner cop-out — it is the control set that survives the hand-off."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not open a second page that is not there. If you bought full Drift, that IAP is Drift, not Wavetable."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load one favorite Wavetable preset.","Move each first-tab knob to an extreme, then back to a musical value.","Double-tap one knob to prove reset.","Swap <strong>FX1</strong> or <strong>FX2</strong> to Reverb or Delay and set a small mix.","Recite: Note Wavetable = these knobs + two FX. That is the instrument."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What should you turn first? <strong>Answer:</strong> The first-tab parameters this preset actually shows.","What survives in Live Standard? <strong>Answer:</strong> The locked-rack macros / shown controls (KB).","Does full Drift unlock Wavetable? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Drift IAP is Drift only (Manual §7.2.1)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1, §7.2, §7.2.4","Using Note Presets in Live"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make two user presets from one factory Wavetable: “bright lead” and “dark lead,” macros only. Save both (7.1.11)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-6-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-6-4","title":"7.6.4 — Wavetable lead","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.6.4 — Wavetable lead","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.6 — Wavetable deep (Note surface)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a lead line on a Wavetable preset using In Key pads or Keys, then stop before you mix."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Beatmaking with Ableton Note: a memorable melody belongs on the hook/chorus. Note’s job is the Session clip, not Arrangement."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pick a Wavetable preset that already cuts (7.6.1 favorites). Enable <strong>In Key</strong> (§5.3) so pads cannot play out-of-scale notes. Keys layout is two octaves (Manual §8.1.3). Capture Fixed or Free (Level 5). Stay inside 16 bars (FAQ)."},{"type":"prose","html":"FX: a short Delay on FX1 is enough. Do not build a Live-style lead chain. Sister course <strong>Sound Design</strong> owns synthesis theory; here you play the Note surface."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load a bright Wavetable preset. Enable <strong>In Key</strong>.","Play a 4-bar hook on pads or Keys. Capture it.","Tweak one first-tab knob so the lead is thinner or brighter. Do not start a mix.","Duplicate the clip (Instrument View Duplicate Clip) if you want a second take.","Recite: lead = one clip, in key, macros only."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why enable In Key for a lead? <strong>Answer:</strong> Melodic pads snap to the scale (Manual §5.3).","What length ceiling applies? <strong>Answer:</strong> 16 bars (FAQ).","Where does deep Wavetable theory live? <strong>Answer:</strong> Not here. Sound Design sister course / Live Suite — not this surface."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.3, §7.2.4, §8.1.3","Note FAQ (16-bar clips)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (memorable hook)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Name the clip in your head after the working title. You will put it on Scene 2 (hook) in the capstone."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-6-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-6-5","title":"7.6.5 — Wavetable bass","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.6.5 — Wavetable bass","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.6 — Wavetable deep (Note surface)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a bass clip on Wavetable (or admit Drift may fit better) without inventing oscillator pages."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Beatmaking with Ableton Note: bass/808 sets the tuning reference for the root. On Note you can do that with a Wavetable bass preset <strong>or</strong> with Drift (module 7.3). New tracks get improved default octaves for bass vs melodic (RN 1.2) — still no published C1/C3 numbers. {type:'gap'}"},{"type":"prose","html":"In Key keeps the bass on scale tones. Play low on the pad grid or drop Octave (Manual §8.1.3). Keep the clip short. Leave headroom on the track fader; Main is system volume plus optional Main FX (RN 1.0.15)."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the Wavetable bass is a locked rack later in Live Standard, the macros you set today are what you will still have (KB)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load a dark Wavetable preset. Play the lowest comfortable in-key pad.","If it is too high, use <strong>Octave</strong> down. Do not invent a note-name guarantee.","Capture a 2- or 4-bar root pattern against the kick.","Tweak one macro for weight. Stop. Do not EQ-carve a Live bass.","Recite: bass = root reference (Beatmaking) + Note macros."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What musical job does bass have in the Beatmaking article? <strong>Answer:</strong> It sets the tuning reference / root.","What RN mentions bass vs melodic octaves? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.2 — “improved default octaves,” no numbers.","Must bass be Wavetable? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Drift is the subtractive alternative (7.3)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note (808/bass)","Note Release Notes 1.2 (default octaves)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (Octave)","Using Note Presets in Live"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play the bass clip with the 7.6.4 lead. If they fight, change one macro or the octave, not the key of the Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-6-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-6-6","title":"7.6.6 — Evolving pad via automation (preview of Level 8)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.6.6 — Evolving pad via automation (preview of Level 8)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.6 — Wavetable deep (Note surface)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture one Wavetable (or Sampler) pad clip, then capture <strong>automation</strong> of a first-tab knob while transport runs."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.5: automation requires <strong>transport running</strong>. Move a device or effect parameter; a <strong>dotted</strong> line appears in Clip Preview; tap <strong>Add</strong> to store it as a <strong>solid</strong> line. Automated knobs show a <strong>dot</strong>. Insert/Delete live in the parameter menu. <strong>Return to Default</strong> is greyed while automation exists."},{"type":"prose","html":"Capture in Note: same rule — transport must run to capture automation."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is a preview of Level 8, not the full automation module. One knob, one clip. A Filter or macro sweep turns a static Wavetable pad into a scene that arrives."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load a Wavetable pad. Capture a held 4-bar chord (In Key).","Press <strong>Play</strong>. Move one first-tab knob slowly across the loop.","When the dotted line looks right, tap <strong>Add</strong>. Confirm the line is solid and the knob has a dot.","Play the clip without touching the knob. Confirm it moves itself.","Recite: transport on → dotted → Add → solid."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Can you capture automation with transport stopped? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Manual §7.5 / Capture KB: transport must run.","What does a dotted line mean? <strong>Answer:</strong> Pending automation. <strong>Add</strong> commits it.","Why is Return to Default grey? <strong>Answer:</strong> The parameter is automated (Manual §7.5.1)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.5 / §7.5.1","Capture in Note (Automation pages)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave this clip in the Set. Level 8 will teach Insert, Delete, and multi-parameter Add. Do not delete this automation until then."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-6-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-6-7","title":"7.6.7 — Content note: some old Wavetable presets were removed","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.6.7 — Content note: some old Wavetable presets were removed","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.6 — Wavetable deep (Note surface)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Read release notes as a content ledger: presets get added and removed; your Set must still open."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note Release Notes <strong>1.1</strong>: “Content: Removed 32 old Wavetable-based presets.” The same notes added Drift racks and other content, and shipped Live 12 Lite to Note users."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is why a tutorial that names a factory preset from 1.0.x may not find it on 1.1+. Favorites and <strong>user presets</strong> (RN 1.0.12; Manual §7.1.2) are how you keep a sound you actually use. Cloud is not a backup of the factory library (FAQ)."},{"type":"prose","html":"If a Set referenced a removed factory preset, {type:'gap'} the extracts do not describe the exact missing-preset dialog. Note what <strong>Help</strong> shows, then replace the track from the current browser."},{"type":"prose","html":"Always log the Note version on the process page (Lesson 0.2.7). Content is versioned."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. Write the version.","Skim the Note release notes for your version (https://www.ableton.com/en/release-notes/note/).","Search the browser for a Wavetable name you remember from an old video. If it is gone, pick a current replacement and favorite it.","Save one user preset of a Wavetable you care about.","Recite: factory content moves; user presets and version notes are the ledger."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What did Note 1.1 remove? <strong>Answer:</strong> 32 old Wavetable-based presets.","How do you keep a sound across updates? <strong>Answer:</strong> Save a user preset (Manual §7.1.2 / RN 1.0.12).","Is Cloud a factory-library archive? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Cloud holds eight Sets for transfer (FAQ)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.1 (removed 32 Wavetable presets)","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (user presets)","Ableton Note Manual §7.1.2","Note FAQ (Cloud is not backup)","https://www.ableton.com/en/release-notes/note/"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make a two-column list: “Factory I used today” / “User preset name.” Update it after every Note update."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-6-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l7-6-8","title":"7.6.8 — Gate G: Drift or Wavetable plus Drum Rack","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.6.8 — Gate G: Drift or Wavetable plus Drum Rack","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 7 · Module 7.6 — Wavetable deep (Note surface)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Self-score Gate G: one kit you designed, plus one Drift <strong>or</strong> Wavetable instrument you can explain from the Note surface."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Gate G is a checkpoint, not a hidden exam. Score yourself against facts this level already sourced."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Pass if all of these are true in one Set:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["A Drum Rack with 16 pads, pad-local parameters understood, at least one Pad FX send or Kit FX you can name (Manual §7.1, §7.2.2, §7.4).","Either <strong>Drift</strong> (subtractive; optional full-Drift IAP documented) <strong>or</strong> <strong>Wavetable</strong> (wavetable oscillators; macros differ per preset; locked rack in Live Standard).","You can state the sampler-path transpose/speed rule and the 2.x audio-track exception.","Track Preset = instrument + two effects.","Note version written from <strong>Help</strong>."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Fail any line, stay in Level 7. Do not invent a software grader."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the Set you will submit for Gate G.","Play the Drum Rack. Point at Pad FX vs Kit FX.","Play the Drift or Wavetable track. Name three knobs actually on screen.","Say the warp conflict in one sentence (7.5.8).","Tick the five lines above on paper. That is Gate G."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Must you show both Drift and Wavetable? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. One synth family plus Drum Rack.","Must full Drift be purchased? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Default Drift subset is enough if you can explain it. IAP is optional (Manual §7.2.1; FAQ also says no IAP historically — version-check the storefront).","Who grades Gate G? <strong>Answer:</strong> You. This app cannot watch Note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1–7.5","Using Note Presets in Live","Note FAQ + RN 2.0.1 (warp paths)","Course syllabus Gate G intent (self-assessment)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Export audio of a 16-bar scene that contains the kit + the synth (Set Settings → Export Audio, only if clips exist). Keep the file with your version number."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l7-6-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
